with r(n, q, answer, exp) as (values
(1, $x$The source of pulmonary surfactant is:$x$, $x$Great alveolar (type II) cells$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Great alveolar (type II) cells
Surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) and proteins made by the type II alveolar cells, also called great alveolar cells or type II pneumocytes. It lines the inside of each alveolus and lowers surface tension, so the small alveoli do not collapse at the end of expiration and the lungs are easier to inflate (better compliance). Type I cells, by contrast, are thin, flat cells that form the gas-exchange surface.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The visceral pleura — The visceral pleura is the membrane covering the outside of the lung; it makes pleural fluid for smooth gliding, not surfactant.
• Tracheal glands — Tracheal glands make mucus that traps dust and germs in the airways; they do not make surfactant.
• Alveolar capillaries — Alveolar capillaries carry blood for gas exchange; they do not secrete anything into the alveoli.

⭐ Key point: Type II pneumocytes make surfactant. Preterm babies (before about 34 weeks) lack it, causing respiratory distress syndrome; antenatal steroids speed up its production.$x$),
(2, $x$Normal arterial partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2) in a healthy adult breathing room air is approximately:$x$, $x$80-100 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 80-100 mmHg
In a healthy adult breathing room air (FiO₂ 21%) at sea level, the normal PaO₂ is 80–100 mmHg (10.7–13.3 kPa). It falls slightly with age. PaO₂ is the pressure of oxygen dissolved in arterial blood, and it drives oxygen onto haemoglobin. Below 80 mmHg is hypoxaemia; below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) is respiratory failure, because the oxygen–haemoglobin curve becomes steep and saturation falls quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40-45 mmHg — 40–45 mmHg is close to the normal VENOUS PO₂ (about 40 mmHg), not arterial.
• 60-65 mmHg — 60–65 mmHg is hypoxaemia; 60 mmHg is the cut-off for respiratory failure.
• 120-140 mmHg — 120–140 mmHg is higher than normal and is only seen when breathing extra oxygen.

⭐ Key point: Normal PaO₂ 80–100 mmHg. PaO₂ below 60 mmHg = respiratory failure (type 1 if PaCO₂ is normal or low; type 2 if PaCO₂ is above 45 mmHg).$x$),
(3, $x$In a healthy person, the primary physiological stimulus that drives minute-to-minute ventilation is:$x$, $x$A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2)
Breathing is controlled mainly by central chemoreceptors in the medulla, which respond to CO₂. CO₂ crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid, forms carbonic acid and lowers the pH there. Even a small rise in PaCO₂ quickly increases the rate and depth of breathing to blow off the extra CO₂. This is the main minute-to-minute drive in a healthy person.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A rise in arterial oxygen tension (PaO2) — A rise in PaO₂ would reduce, not drive, breathing. Low oxygen (below about 60 mmHg) is only a back-up drive, sensed by the carotid and aortic bodies.
• A fall in blood glucose — Blood glucose does not directly control breathing.
• A rise in serum calcium — Serum calcium affects nerves and muscles but is not a breathing stimulus.

⭐ Key point: CO₂ is the main drive to breathe. In chronic CO₂ retainers (some COPD patients), the hypoxic drive matters more, so give controlled oxygen with a target SpO₂ of 88–92%.$x$),
(4, $x$Which of the following causes a leftward shift of the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve (increased affinity for oxygen)?$x$, $x$Decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG, and alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG, and alkalosis
A LEFT shift means haemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly (higher affinity), so it picks up O₂ easily in the lungs but releases less to the tissues. Causes of a left shift: LOW temperature, LOW 2,3-DPG, ALKALOSIS (high pH), LOW CO₂, and also carbon monoxide and fetal haemoglobin. This option lists three left-shift causes together, so it is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased temperature — Increased temperature shifts the curve to the RIGHT (hot, working tissues need more oxygen released).
• Decreased pH (acidosis) — Acidosis (low pH) shifts the curve to the RIGHT (the Bohr effect).
• Increased 2,3-DPG — Increased 2,3-DPG shifts the curve to the RIGHT (as in anaemia and high altitude).

⭐ Key point: Remember "CADET, face Right": CO₂, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise and Temperature all shift the curve to the RIGHT (more O₂ released). The opposite of each shifts it LEFT.$x$),
(5, $x$Which patient can safely receive AB-negative blood?$x$, $x$AB-negative$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: AB-negative
AB-negative red cells carry the A antigen, the B antigen, and no D (Rh) antigen. They can only be given safely to someone whose plasma has NO anti-A and NO anti-B antibodies, which means a group AB recipient. Because the cells are Rh-negative, they can go to an AB-negative (or AB-positive) patient. Among the options, only the AB-negative patient can safely receive them.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• O-negative — An O-negative patient has both anti-A and anti-B, which would destroy AB cells (a haemolytic reaction).
• B-negative — A B-negative patient has anti-A, which would attack the A antigen on AB cells.
• A-negative — An A-negative patient has anti-B, which would attack the B antigen on AB cells.

⭐ Key point: Red cells: O-negative is the universal donor and AB-positive is the universal recipient. Plasma is the reverse: AB plasma is the universal plasma donor.$x$),
(6, $x$The average lifespan of a red blood cell is approximately:$x$, $x$120 days$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120 days
Red blood cells live for about 120 days. They have no nucleus, so they cannot repair themselves. As they age, they become stiff and are removed by macrophages in the spleen (and the liver). The haem is turned into bilirubin, and the iron is recycled to make new red cells in the bone marrow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 days — 10 days is about the lifespan of platelets (7–10 days).
• 40 days — 40 days is too short for a red cell.
• 300 days — 300 days is far too long; no blood cell lives this long in the circulation.

⭐ Key point: Red cells live 120 days, platelets 7–10 days, and neutrophils only hours to days. Stored red cells for transfusion last 35–42 days in the fridge.$x$),
(7, $x$Haemophilia A results from a deficiency of which clotting factor?$x$, $x$Factor VIII$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Factor VIII
Haemophilia A is an X-linked inherited bleeding disorder caused by a lack of clotting factor VIII. It mostly affects males; females are usually carriers. Patients bleed into joints (haemarthrosis) and muscles. The blood test shows a prolonged APTT with a normal PT and platelet count. Treatment is factor VIII concentrate (or desmopressin in mild cases).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Factor IX — Factor IX deficiency is haemophilia B (Christmas disease).
• Factor XI — Factor XI deficiency is haemophilia C, a milder form.
• Factor XIII — Factor XIII deficiency is a rare disorder of clot stabilisation, not haemophilia A.

⭐ Key point: Haemophilia A = factor VIII; B = factor IX. Avoid IM injections, aspirin and NSAIDs, and give the factor BEFORE procedures.$x$),
(8, $x$The AV node's primary function in the cardiac conduction system is to:$x$, $x$Briefly delay the electrical impulse, allowing the atria to fully contract before ventricular contraction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Briefly delay the electrical impulse, allowing the atria to fully contract before ventricular contraction
The AV (atrioventricular) node sits between the atria and ventricles. Its main job is to DELAY the impulse for about 0.1 second. This gives the atria time to finish contracting and fill the ventricles before the ventricles contract. The delay appears on the ECG as the PR interval (normal 0.12–0.20 s). The AV node also protects the ventricles from very fast atrial rates, as in AF.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generate the primary pacemaker impulse — Generating the primary pacemaker impulse is the job of the SA node (60–100/min).
• Directly cause ventricular contraction without delay — The impulse must be delayed, not passed straight to the ventricles; the bundle of His and Purkinje fibres conduct it rapidly afterwards.
• Regulate coronary blood flow — Coronary blood flow is controlled by the coronary arteries and local metabolites, not the AV node.

⭐ Key point: SA node = pacemaker; AV node = delay (PR interval). If the SA node fails, the AV junction can take over at 40–60/min.$x$),
(9, $x$Which nephron segment is primarily responsible for the countercurrent multiplier system that concentrates urine?$x$, $x$Loop of Henle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Loop of Henle
The loop of Henle (in the medulla) is the countercurrent multiplier. The descending limb lets water out; the thick ascending limb pumps sodium and chloride out but is impermeable to water. This builds a very salty (hyperosmolar) medulla. Then, under ADH, water leaves the collecting ducts into the salty medulla and the urine becomes concentrated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bowman's capsule — Bowman's capsule collects the filtrate from the glomerulus; it does not concentrate urine.
• Proximal convoluted tubule — The proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs about 65% of filtered sodium and water and all glucose, but the fluid stays isotonic.
• Renal pelvis — The renal pelvis is a collecting funnel that carries urine to the ureter.

⭐ Key point: Loop of Henle = concentrates urine. Loop diuretics (furosemide) block the Na-K-2Cl pump in the thick ascending limb, which is why they are so powerful.$x$),
(10, $x$Antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released into the circulation from the:$x$, $x$Posterior pituitary gland$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Posterior pituitary gland
ADH (vasopressin) is MADE in the hypothalamus (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei). It travels down nerve fibres to the POSTERIOR pituitary, where it is stored and released into the blood. ADH makes the collecting ducts keep water, so it raises blood volume and concentrates the urine. Oxytocin is handled the same way.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior pituitary gland — The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones (GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin), not ADH.
• Adrenal medulla — The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline.
• Thyroid gland — The thyroid gland releases T3, T4 and calcitonin.

⭐ Key point: Too little ADH = diabetes insipidus (large volumes of dilute urine). Too much ADH = SIADH (low sodium, concentrated urine). Both are common after head injury and brain surgery.$x$),
(11, $x$The normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in a healthy adult is approximately:$x$, $x$120-125 mL/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120-125 mL/min
Normal GFR is about 120–125 mL/min (about 180 L/day) in a healthy young adult. Almost all of this filtrate (about 99%) is reabsorbed, leaving 1–2 L of urine a day. GFR is the best overall measure of kidney function; it is estimated (eGFR) from serum creatinine, age and sex.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20-30 mL/min — 20–30 mL/min is severely reduced (stage 4 CKD).
• 500 mL/min — 500 mL/min is about the renal plasma flow, not the GFR.
• 1000 mL/min — 1000 mL/min is close to total renal BLOOD flow (about 20% of cardiac output), not the filtration rate.

⭐ Key point: GFR normal ~120 mL/min. Stage 5 CKD (kidney failure) is GFR below 15. Many drugs (vancomycin, gentamicin, enoxaparin) need dose changes when GFR is low.$x$),
(12, $x$Cerebrospinal fluid circulates within the$x$, $x$subarachnoid space$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: subarachnoid space
CSF flows in the SUBARACHNOID space, between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, and in the ventricles and central canal. It is made mainly by the choroid plexus (about 500 mL/day; about 150 mL present at a time) and absorbed by the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses. It cushions the brain and removes waste. A lumbar puncture samples CSF from this space below L1–L2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• epidural space — The epidural space lies outside the dura and contains fat and veins; epidural anaesthesia goes here, not CSF.
• subdural space — The subdural space lies between the dura and arachnoid; a subdural haematoma collects here.
• potential space between periosteum and dura — The space between the periosteum and dura is the epidural space in the skull, where an extradural haematoma forms.

⭐ Key point: Subarachnoid = CSF. A subarachnoid haemorrhage puts blood into the CSF, giving a thunderclap headache and neck stiffness.$x$),
(13, $x$A patient presents with ptosis, a pupil that is fixed and dilated, and the eye deviated "down and out." Which cranial nerve palsy best explains this presentation?$x$, $x$Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)
The oculomotor nerve (CN III) moves most eye muscles, lifts the eyelid (levator palpebrae) and carries parasympathetic fibres that constrict the pupil. When it is paralysed, the unopposed lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV) pull the eye "down and out". The lid droops (ptosis), and the pupil is fixed and dilated. In head injury, a new dilated pupil means the uncus is herniating and pressing on CN III.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cranial nerve IV (trochlear) — A CN IV (trochlear) palsy causes vertical double vision, especially going downstairs, without ptosis or a dilated pupil.
• Cranial nerve VI (abducens) — A CN VI (abducens) palsy stops the eye moving outwards; the eye turns in.
• Cranial nerve VII (facial) — A CN VII (facial) palsy causes facial weakness and inability to close the eye, not ptosis with a dilated pupil.

⭐ Key point: A fixed dilated pupil on one side after head injury is a NEUROSURGICAL EMERGENCY (raised ICP and herniation). Report it at once.$x$),
(14, $x$Beta cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans secrete:$x$, $x$Insulin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insulin
In the islets of Langerhans, BETA cells make insulin, which lowers blood glucose by moving it into cells and storing it as glycogen and fat. Beta cells are the most common islet cells (about 70%). They are destroyed in type 1 diabetes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucagon — Glucagon is made by ALPHA cells; it raises blood glucose.
• Somatostatin — Somatostatin is made by DELTA cells; it inhibits insulin and glucagon release.
• Pancreatic polypeptide exclusively — Pancreatic polypeptide is made by PP (F) cells, not beta cells.

⭐ Key point: Beta = insulin (lowers glucose). Alpha = glucagon (raises glucose). Glucagon 1 mg IM is used for severe hypoglycaemia when there is no IV access.$x$),
(15, $x$Intrinsic factor, secreted by gastric parietal cells, is essential for absorption of which nutrient?$x$, $x$Vitamin B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vitamin B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum
Gastric parietal cells make intrinsic factor (as well as hydrochloric acid). Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12, and the complex is absorbed in the TERMINAL ILEUM. Without intrinsic factor (pernicious anaemia, gastrectomy) or without the terminal ileum (Crohn's disease, resection), B12 is not absorbed. The result is macrocytic anaemia and nerve damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Iron, absorbed in the duodenum — Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum, helped by stomach acid and vitamin C, but it does not need intrinsic factor.
• Calcium, absorbed in the jejunum — Calcium is absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum with the help of vitamin D, not intrinsic factor.
• Vitamin K, absorbed in the colon — Vitamin K is partly made by gut bacteria and absorbed with fats; it does not need intrinsic factor.

⭐ Key point: No intrinsic factor = no B12 = pernicious anaemia, which is treated with lifelong IM vitamin B12 (hydroxocobalamin) injections.$x$),
(16, $x$What is the normal range for HCO3-?$x$, $x$22-26 mEq/L$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 22-26 mEq/L
Normal serum bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) is 22–26 mEq/L (mmol/L). Bicarbonate is the main base (alkali) buffer in the blood, and the kidneys control it. Below 22 = metabolic acidosis (or compensation for respiratory alkalosis). Above 26 = metabolic alkalosis (or compensation for respiratory acidosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18-22 mEq/L — 18–22 is low (acidosis range).
• 26-30 mEq/L — 26–30 is high (alkalosis or compensation).
• 30-34 mEq/L — 30–34 is clearly raised, as in vomiting or chronic CO₂ retention with compensation.

⭐ Key point: ABG normals: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 22–26 mmol/L. Remember "the lungs control CO₂ (acid), the kidneys control HCO₃⁻ (base)".$x$),
(17, $x$An ABG result of pH 7.51, PaCO2 35 mmHg, and HCO3- 30 mEq/L indicates:$x$, $x$Metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis
Step 1: pH 7.51 is above 7.45, so the patient has alkalosis. Step 2: PaCO₂ 35 is normal, so the lungs are not the cause. Step 3: HCO₃⁻ 30 is above 26, so there is too much base. The bicarbonate moves in the same direction as the pH (both up), which means a METABOLIC cause: uncompensated metabolic alkalosis. ROME: Metabolic = Equal (pH and HCO₃⁻ move the same way).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would need a LOW pH and a LOW HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would need a high pH with a LOW PaCO₂ (below 35, from hyperventilation).
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would need a LOW pH with a HIGH PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Causes of metabolic alkalosis: vomiting or NG suction (loss of stomach acid), diuretics, low potassium and too much bicarbonate. Treat the cause and replace chloride and potassium.$x$),
(18, $x$In a patient with respiratory failure, the nurse correctly interprets a high PaCO₂ value and a low pH from an ABG. This indicates:$x$, $x$Respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory acidosis
A LOW pH means acidosis. A HIGH PaCO₂ (above 45 mmHg) means CO₂, the respiratory acid, is building up. pH and PaCO₂ move in OPPOSITE directions, so the cause is respiratory. Together they mean respiratory acidosis, which is the typical result of hypoventilation in respiratory failure (type 2): COPD, opioid overdose, exhaustion, neuromuscular weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and a HIGH HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and a LOW PaCO₂ (hyperventilation).
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would show a low pH but a LOW HCO₃⁻ as the main change, with a normal or low PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory acidosis is treated by improving ventilation: open the airway, reverse sedatives (naloxone), use NIV (BiPAP), or intubate and ventilate. The kidneys raise HCO₃⁻ to compensate over 2–5 days.$x$),
(19, $x$What is a cause of metabolic alkalosis?$x$, $x$Vomiting$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vomiting
Vomiting (and NG suction) removes hydrochloric acid (HCl) from the stomach. Losing acid leaves extra bicarbonate behind, which raises the pH. Loss of fluid and chloride makes the kidneys hold on to more bicarbonate, and potassium is lost too. The result is a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, as in pyloric stenosis in babies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diarrhea — Diarrhoea causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, because bowel fluid is rich in bicarbonate, which is lost.
• Renal failure — Renal failure causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, because the kidneys cannot excrete acid or make bicarbonate.
• Hypoventilation — Hypoventilation causes RESPIRATORY acidosis (CO₂ builds up).

⭐ Key point: Above the stomach (vomiting) = alkalosis; below the stomach (diarrhoea) = acidosis. Treat with 0.9% saline and potassium.$x$),
(20, $x$The modified Allen's test is performed before arterial blood gas sampling primarily to:$x$, $x$Assess collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery before radial artery puncture$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery before radial artery puncture
The radial artery is the usual ABG site. Before puncturing it, the modified Allen test checks that the ULNAR artery can supply the hand on its own if the radial artery is damaged or clotted. Method: press both arteries, ask the patient to clench the fist until the hand blanches, then release only the ulnar artery. Colour should return within 5–15 seconds. If it doesn't, choose another site.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure blood pressure — Blood pressure is measured with a cuff or arterial line, not the Allen test.
• Assess for infection at the puncture site — Infection at the site is checked by looking for redness, swelling and pus.
• Determine the patient's pain tolerance — Pain tolerance is not what the Allen test measures.

⭐ Key point: A negative (abnormal) Allen test means don't use that radial artery. After an ABG, press firmly for at least 5 minutes (longer if the patient is on anticoagulants).$x$),
(21, $x$A patient develops fever with chills and rigors shortly after a blood transfusion is started post-operatively. What is the priority nursing action?$x$, $x$Stop the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with normal saline, notify the physician, and send the blood/tubing for investigation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with normal saline, notify the physician, and send the blood/tubing for investigation
Fever, chills and rigors soon after starting blood may be the first sign of a serious reaction: an acute haemolytic reaction (wrong blood), bacterial contamination, or a febrile reaction. The nurse cannot tell which at the bedside, so the SAFEST first action is to STOP the transfusion at once. Keep the vein open with a new giving set and 0.9% saline, check vital signs, inform the doctor, recheck the patient ID against the bag, and return the bag and tubing with blood and urine samples to the lab.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No action is needed if the temperature is below 38.5 degrees C — Waiting for the temperature to reach a certain level could let a fatal haemolytic reaction continue.
• Increase the transfusion rate to finish it quickly — Speeding up the transfusion gives MORE of the possibly harmful blood.
• Give an antipyretic and continue the transfusion at the same rate — An antipyretic can hide the fever, and continuing the transfusion is unsafe until the cause is known.

⭐ Key point: Any reaction means STOP the blood, keep the line open with saline, call the doctor, check the ID, and send the bag back. Most acute haemolytic reactions are caused by giving blood to the wrong patient.$x$),
(22, $x$Massive blood transfusion is defined as:$x$, $x$Replacement of the patient's total blood volume within 24 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Replacement of the patient's total blood volume within 24 hours
Massive transfusion is usually defined as replacing the patient's whole blood volume (about 70 mL/kg; roughly 10 units of red cells in an adult) within 24 hours. Other definitions include 50% of blood volume within 3 hours, or more than 4 units within 1 hour. It is used in major trauma, PPH and GI bleeding, with a balanced ratio of red cells, FFP and platelets.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 350 mL in 5 minutes — 350 mL in 5 minutes is a rapid infusion, but it is not the definition.
• 500 mL in 5 minutes — 500 mL in 5 minutes is not a recognised definition.
• 1 litre in 5 minutes — 1 litre in 5 minutes is very fast, but the definition is based on blood volume over time.

⭐ Key point: Watch for the complications of massive transfusion: hypothermia, LOW calcium (citrate), HIGH potassium, acidosis and clotting problems. Use a blood warmer and give calcium.$x$),
(23, $x$A patient is admitted to ICU with diagnosis of vaso-occlusive crisis, which of this management will you start with?$x$, $x$Rehydrate with normal saline$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rehydrate with normal saline
A sickle cell vaso-occlusive (pain) crisis happens when sickled cells block small vessels. Dehydration makes the blood thicker and causes more sickling, so the first step is IV REHYDRATION (with normal saline). Rehydration improves blood flow and stops the cycle. Strong pain relief (often opioids) within 30 minutes, oxygen if SpO₂ is low, warmth, and treatment of infection go alongside it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfuse with fresh whole blood — Transfusion is not first-line for an uncomplicated pain crisis; it is used for acute chest syndrome, stroke, or severe anaemia (sequestration). Fresh whole blood is rarely used.
• Order for narcotics and administer — Pain relief is essential and urgent, but rehydration is the first treatment this question is testing.
• Give oral rehydration solution — Oral rehydration solution is too slow and not suitable for an ICU patient in crisis.

⭐ Key point: Sickle crisis care: fluids, oxygen, warmth, pain relief within 30 minutes, and antibiotics for infection. Avoid cold, dehydration and hypoxia, because each one triggers more sickling.$x$),
(24, $x$Laboratory findings consistent with disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) include:$x$, $x$Prolonged PT/aPTT, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prolonged PT/aPTT, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer
In DIC, small clots form throughout the body. These use up platelets and clotting factors, so the patient then BLEEDS from everywhere: line sites, gums, wounds. Laboratory findings: PT and APTT PROLONGED (factors used up), platelets LOW, fibrinogen LOW, and D-dimer HIGH (clot breakdown). Causes include sepsis, trauma, placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism and HELLP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shortened PT/aPTT and elevated platelets — Shortened clotting times with high platelets do not fit DIC, which consumes clotting factors.
• Normal fibrinogen and normal D-dimer — Normal fibrinogen and D-dimer rule DIC out.
• Isolated elevated hemoglobin — Haemoglobin usually FALLS in DIC (bleeding and haemolysis); a raised haemoglobin alone is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause. Replace with platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate (for low fibrinogen) if the patient is bleeding.$x$),
(25, $x$Which of the following pathogens is a common cause of ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) in the ICU?$x$, $x$Pseudomonas aeruginosa$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pseudomonas aeruginosa
Pseudomonas aeruginosa is one of the commonest causes of VAP, especially late VAP (after 5 or more days on the ventilator). It is a Gram-negative bacterium that lives in moist places (sinks, ventilator circuits, humidifiers) and is often resistant to many antibiotics. Other VAP germs: Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, and E. coli.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Common cold rhinovirus only — Rhinovirus causes the common cold, not typical VAP.
• Malaria parasite — The malaria parasite infects red blood cells and liver cells, not the lungs via the ventilator.
• Tapeworm — Tapeworm is a gut parasite and has nothing to do with VAP.

⭐ Key point: Prevent VAP with the bundle: head up 30–45°, daily sedation hold, chlorhexidine mouth care, subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, and hand hygiene.$x$),
(26, $x$Autoclaving is a sterilisation method that primarily uses:$x$, $x$Pressurised steam at high temperature$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pressurised steam at high temperature
An autoclave sterilises with STEAM UNDER PRESSURE. Pressure raises the boiling point, so steam reaches 121 °C (held for 15 minutes) or 134 °C (held for 3 minutes). Moist heat kills ALL microorganisms, including bacterial spores, by denaturing their proteins. It is the commonest, cheapest and most reliable method for metal instruments, linen and dressings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ultraviolet light only — Ultraviolet light only reduces germs on surfaces and in air; it does not sterilise instruments.
• Cold chemical soaking only, with no heat — Cold chemical soaking (such as 2% glutaraldehyde) is high-level disinfection, used for heat-sensitive items, not autoclaving.
• Dry air at room temperature — Dry air at room temperature does not kill microorganisms.

⭐ Key point: Autoclave: 121 °C for 15 minutes or 134 °C for 3 minutes. Clean items first; check indicator tape, and use biological indicators (spore tests) to prove sterility.$x$),
(27, $x$To reduce contamination risk, blood cultures should ideally be drawn:$x$, $x$From two separate sites, ideally before starting antibiotics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: From two separate sites, ideally before starting antibiotics
Taking blood cultures from TWO separate sites (two sets) helps tell a true infection from skin contamination. If the same germ grows from both sites, it is real; if only one bottle grows a skin germ, it is probably a contaminant. Cultures must be taken BEFORE antibiotics, because even one dose can stop the germs growing in the bottle and give a false negative. Clean the skin with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol and let it dry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• From a single site only, after antibiotics are given — Taking cultures after antibiotics, or from only one site, makes the results unreliable.
• Only from a central line hub — Using only a central line hub increases contamination (unless line infection is suspected, and then a peripheral set is also taken).
• Without skin antisepsis — Skipping skin antisepsis guarantees contamination with skin bacteria.

⭐ Key point: In sepsis: cultures FIRST (two sets), then antibiotics within 1 hour. Never delay antibiotics for long just to get cultures.$x$),
(28, $x$What are the two key pharmacological antidotes used in the management of organophosphate poisoning?$x$, $x$Atropine and pralidoxime$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atropine and pralidoxime
Organophosphates (insecticides such as Sniper/DDVP) block the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up. This causes SLUDGE signs (salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, GI cramps, emesis), small pupils, slow pulse, bronchorrhoea (wet chest), and muscle twitching and weakness. ATROPINE blocks the muscarinic effects and dries the chest (give it until the chest is clear). PRALIDOXIME re-activates the enzyme (helps the muscle weakness) if given early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone and flumazenil — Naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines, not organophosphates.
• Atropine and naloxone — Naloxone has no role here; only atropine is correct in that pair.
• Pralidoxime and N-acetylcysteine — N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for paracetamol; only pralidoxime is correct in that pair.

⭐ Key point: Organophosphate care: remove contaminated clothes, wash the skin (wear PPE yourself), protect the airway, then give large doses of atropine plus pralidoxime.$x$),
(29, $x$Protamine sulphate is antidote of$x$, $x$Heparin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Heparin
Protamine sulphate reverses HEPARIN. Protamine is strongly positive (basic) and heparin is strongly negative (acidic), so they bind and neutralise each other. 1 mg of protamine reverses about 100 units of heparin given in the last 2–3 hours. It only partly reverses LMWH (enoxaparin). Give it slowly IV; fast injection causes low BP, slow pulse and anaphylaxis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ephedrine — Ephedrine is a vasopressor for low BP during anaesthesia; it has no protamine antidote.
• Pethadine — Pethidine is an opioid; its antidote is naloxone.
• Scoline — Scoline (suxamethonium) is a muscle relaxant; it has no specific antidote (prolonged block is managed by ventilation).

⭐ Key point: Heparin → protamine. Warfarin → vitamin K plus PCC or FFP. Opioids → naloxone. Benzodiazepines → flumazenil.$x$),
(30, $x$Specific antidote in paracetamol poisoning is$x$, $x$Acetyl cysteine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acetyl cysteine
N-acetylcysteine (NAC, Parvolex) is the specific antidote for paracetamol overdose. In overdose, a toxic metabolite (NAPQI) builds up and destroys liver cells. NAC refills glutathione, which neutralises NAPQI. It works best within 8 hours of ingestion but is still given later. Treatment is guided by the paracetamol blood level at 4 hours or more after ingestion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Desferroxamine — Desferrioxamine is the antidote for iron poisoning.
• Naloxone — Naloxone is the antidote for opioids.
• Activated charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces absorption of many drugs within 1 hour, but it is not a specific antidote.

⭐ Key point: Paracetamol liver damage may not show for 24–72 hours, so start NAC early, check LFTs and INR, and watch for low glucose and liver failure.$x$),
(31, $x$First drug used to treat bradycardia:$x$, $x$Atropine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atropine
For symptomatic bradycardia (low BP, chest pain, shock, syncope, heart failure), the first drug is ATROPINE 500 micrograms IV, repeated up to a total of 3 mg. Atropine blocks the vagus nerve, which speeds up the SA node and AV conduction. If atropine fails, use transcutaneous pacing or adrenaline/isoprenaline infusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Epinephrine — Epinephrine (adrenaline) is a second-line infusion if atropine fails, or for cardiac arrest.
• Amiodarone — Amiodarone treats FAST rhythms (VT, AF) and can slow the heart further.
• Deltacortil — Deltacortil (prednisolone) is a steroid and has no role in bradycardia.

⭐ Key point: Atropine may not work in a heart transplant patient (no vagal supply) or in complete heart block with wide QRS; prepare for pacing.$x$),
(32, $x$A patient on a high-dose, prolonged sodium nitroprusside infusion develops altered mental status and unexplained metabolic acidosis. This should raise suspicion for:$x$, $x$Cyanide toxicity from accumulation of nitroprusside metabolites$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cyanide toxicity from accumulation of nitroprusside metabolites
Sodium nitroprusside contains cyanide. When high doses run for a long time, especially with kidney or liver problems, cyanide (and thiocyanate) builds up. Cyanide stops cells using oxygen, causing confusion, metabolic (lactic) acidosis, a rising mixed venous oxygen level (SvO₂), arrhythmias and eventually death. Stop the infusion and give an antidote (hydroxocobalamin or sodium thiosulphate).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome is caused by serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, tramadol, MDMA), not nitroprusside.
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia is caused by volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium.
• Digoxin toxicity — Digoxin toxicity causes nausea, visual changes and arrhythmias, not this picture with nitroprusside.

⭐ Key point: Protect nitroprusside from light (cover the syringe and line), use the lowest dose for the shortest time, and check lactate and acid–base status.$x$),
(33, $x$The objective of medical management of angina is to:$x$, $x$Decrease myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decrease myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply
Angina is chest pain caused by the heart muscle needing more oxygen than the narrowed coronary arteries can supply. Treatment aims to REDUCE oxygen DEMAND and INCREASE oxygen SUPPLY. Beta-blockers slow the heart rate and nitrates reduce preload (lower demand); nitrates and calcium channel blockers widen the coronary arteries (more supply); aspirin and statins prevent clots and plaque growth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply — Increasing demand would make the imbalance and the pain worse.
• Increase demand and decrease supply — Increasing demand and decreasing supply is the exact cause of ischaemia, not its treatment.
• Maintain the general circulation — Maintaining the general circulation is too vague and doesn't describe the specific aim.

⭐ Key point: For angina, rest plus GTN sublingual. If pain lasts more than 20 minutes or doesn't respond, treat it as ACS: ECG, troponin and aspirin.$x$),
(34, $x$Which of these drugs is used as an antidote for narcotics$x$, $x$Nalaxone$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nalaxone
Naloxone is the specific antagonist (antidote) for opioids (narcotics such as morphine, heroin, pethidine, fentanyl and tramadol). It blocks opioid receptors and reverses respiratory depression, sedation and small pupils within 1–2 minutes when given IV. Dose: 0.4 mg IV, repeated every 2–3 minutes. Smaller doses (40–100 mcg) are used in post-op patients so pain relief is not completely reversed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Black charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces gut absorption of many oral poisons but does not reverse opioids.
• Atropine — Atropine is the antidote for organophosphates and treats bradycardia.
• Cimetidine — Cimetidine is an H₂ blocker that lowers stomach acid; it is not an antidote.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone works for a SHORTER time (30–90 minutes) than many opioids, so the patient can become sedated again (re-narcotisation). Monitor closely and repeat doses or start an infusion.$x$),
(35, $x$The preferred muscle relaxant in a patient with liver or renal failure is:$x$, $x$Atracurium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atracurium
Atracurium is broken down in the blood by Hofmann elimination (a spontaneous chemical breakdown at normal body temperature and pH) and by ester hydrolysis, NOT by the liver or kidneys. Its action therefore stays predictable in liver or kidney failure. Cisatracurium works the same way and is often preferred in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rocuronium — Rocuronium is cleared mainly by the liver (and kidneys), so it can last longer in liver failure.
• Pancuronium — Pancuronium is mainly cleared by the kidneys and is very long-acting in renal failure.
• Vecuronium — Vecuronium depends on the liver and kidneys, and its active metabolite builds up in organ failure.

⭐ Key point: Atracurium and cisatracurium are the muscle relaxants of choice in liver or kidney failure. Note that atracurium can release histamine (rash, low BP).$x$),
(36, $x$A patient sedated with a continuous propofol infusion for several days develops metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, and cardiac dysfunction. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Propofol Infusion Syndrome, a rare but serious complication of prolonged high-dose propofol use$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Propofol Infusion Syndrome, a rare but serious complication of prolonged high-dose propofol use
Propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS) is a rare but often fatal complication of high-dose (above 4 mg/kg/h) propofol given for more than 48 hours, especially in children, sepsis, head injury and with steroids or catecholamines. It disrupts how the mitochondria burn fat. Signs: unexplained metabolic (lactic) acidosis, rhabdomyolysis (high CK), high potassium, high triglycerides, kidney failure, arrhythmias and heart failure. Stop propofol at once and give supportive care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal, expected sedative effect with no concern — These are dangerous abnormalities, not a normal sedative effect.
• A simple allergic reaction to propofol — An allergic reaction causes rash, wheeze and low BP, not acidosis with rhabdomyolysis.
• Improved sedation with no adverse effect — This picture is harmful; it does not mean better sedation.

⭐ Key point: With long propofol infusions, monitor triglycerides, lactate, CK and ECG, and keep the dose below 4 mg/kg/h where possible.$x$),
(37, $x$A patient with a major burn receives morphine IV. The nurse prioritizes monitoring for:$x$, $x$Respiratory depression$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory depression
Morphine, like all opioids, depresses the respiratory centre in the brainstem, causing slow, shallow breathing, CO₂ retention, low oxygen and apnoea. This is the most dangerous side effect and can be fatal, especially with IV doses, in burns patients who may also have smoke inhalation, and when combined with sedatives. The nurse monitors respiratory rate, depth, SpO₂ and sedation level and keeps naloxone ready.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension — Morphine is more likely to cause LOW blood pressure (vasodilation), not high.
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is a side effect but not life-threatening.
• Constipation — Constipation is common but not the priority in the acute setting.

⭐ Key point: Opioid danger signs are a respiratory rate below 8–10, increasing sleepiness and pinpoint pupils. Sedation usually comes before respiratory depression, so check the sedation score.$x$),
(38, $x$A patient is prescribed dopamine 5 mcg/kg/min. The patient weighs 70 kg and the infusion is mixed as 400 mg in 250 mL D5W. What is the infusion rate in mL/hr?$x$, $x$13.1 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 13.1 mL/hr
Step 1, dose per minute: 5 mcg × 70 kg = 350 mcg/min. Step 2, dose per hour: 350 × 60 = 21,000 mcg/h = 21 mg/h. Step 3, concentration: 400 mg ÷ 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL. Step 4, rate: 21 mg/h ÷ 1.6 mg/mL = 13.1 mL/h. Shortcut formula: (dose mcg/kg/min × weight × 60) ÷ concentration in mcg/mL = (5 × 70 × 60) ÷ 1600 = 13.1 mL/h.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10.5 mL/hr — 10.5 mL/h comes from a calculation error, such as using the wrong weight or concentration.
• 21.9 mL/hr — 21.9 mL/h comes from mixing up mg and mL or using the wrong concentration.
• 26.3 mL/hr — 26.3 mL/h is about double the right answer, a common error from using a weaker concentration.

⭐ Key point: Always convert to the SAME units (mcg or mg) before dividing. Have a second nurse check every vasoactive infusion calculation.$x$),
(39, $x$A litre of Normal Saline is ordered to run over 8 hours via a gravity administration set with a drop factor of 15 gtt/mL. What is the correct flow rate in drops per minute?$x$, $x$31 gtt/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 31 gtt/min
Formula: drops per minute = (volume in mL × drop factor) ÷ time in MINUTES. Volume 1000 mL; drop factor 15 gtt/mL; time 8 hours = 480 minutes. (1000 × 15) ÷ 480 = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25, which rounds to 31 drops per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 21 gtt/min — 21 gtt/min would give too little fluid; it comes from a wrong drop factor or time.
• 42 gtt/min — 42 gtt/min is the answer for a 20 gtt/mL set (1000 × 20 ÷ 480 = 41.7), not 15.
• 15 gtt/min — 15 gtt/min just repeats the drop factor and is not a calculation.

⭐ Key point: Standard giving sets are 20 drops/mL (adult crystalloid), blood sets 15 drops/mL, and paediatric (burette/micro-drip) sets 60 drops/mL. With a 60-drop set, drops per minute = mL per hour.$x$),
(40, $x$Activated charcoal is most effective in acute poisoning management when given:$x$, $x$Early after ingestion (typically within about 1 hour)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Early after ingestion (typically within about 1 hour)
Activated charcoal binds (adsorbs) many drugs in the gut so they are not absorbed. It works best when given EARLY, ideally within 1 hour of swallowing a poison, because after that much of the drug has already been absorbed. The adult dose is 50 g orally (or via NG tube) and the child dose is 1 g/kg. The patient must have a protected airway or be awake, because aspiration is dangerous.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• More than 24 hours after ingestion — After 24 hours almost all of the drug is absorbed, so charcoal does little (except for some slow-release drugs).
• Only intravenously — Charcoal is given by MOUTH or NG tube, never IV.
• Only for iron overdose — Charcoal does NOT bind iron (or lithium, alcohol, acids or alkalis).

⭐ Key point: Don't use charcoal for iron, lithium, alcohols, corrosives or hydrocarbons such as kerosene, or in a patient who cannot protect their airway.$x$),
(41, $x$A classic chest X-ray sign of pleural effusion is:$x$, $x$Blunting of the costophrenic angle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blunting of the costophrenic angle
Fluid in the pleural space collects at the lowest point first. On an erect chest X-ray, the sharp costophrenic angle (where the diaphragm meets the ribs) becomes blunted, often with a curved upper edge (meniscus sign). At least about 200–300 mL is needed to show on a PA film. A large effusion makes the whole lower zone white and can push the trachea and mediastinum to the opposite side.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperinflated lung fields — Hyperinflated (black, enlarged) lung fields suggest COPD or asthma, not effusion.
• A widened mediastinum — A widened mediastinum suggests aortic injury or dissection.
• Free air under the diaphragm — Free air under the diaphragm means a perforated bowel or stomach.

⭐ Key point: An effusion is dull to percussion, with reduced breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance. Large ones are drained by thoracentesis or a chest drain.$x$),
(42, $x$A key chest X-ray sign of pneumothorax is:$x$, $x$Absence of lung markings peripheral to a visible pleural line$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Absence of lung markings peripheral to a visible pleural line
In a pneumothorax, air enters the pleural space and the lung collapses inwards. On X-ray you see a thin white line (the visceral pleural edge), and OUTSIDE it there are NO lung markings (black space with no blood vessels). On a supine ICU film, look for the deep sulcus sign (a deep, dark costophrenic angle).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased lung markings throughout — Increased lung markings suggest fluid, infection or fibrosis, not pneumothorax.
• Bilateral diffuse infiltrates — Bilateral diffuse infiltrates suggest ARDS, pulmonary oedema or pneumonia.
• Widened mediastinum — A widened mediastinum suggests aortic injury.

⭐ Key point: TENSION pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis (distress, low BP, trachea deviated away, absent breath sounds). Decompress with a needle immediately; don't wait for an X-ray.$x$),
(43, $x$On a plain abdominal X-ray, multiple dilated bowel loops with air-fluid levels on an erect film are classic findings of:$x$, $x$Intestinal obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intestinal obstruction
In intestinal obstruction, gas and fluid build up above the blockage. The bowel loops stretch (dilated loops), and on an ERECT film, gas sits on top of fluid, forming flat lines called air–fluid levels (like a glass of water). Small bowel loops are central with folds that cross the whole width (valvulae conniventes); large bowel loops are peripheral with folds that cross only partly (haustra).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal bowel gas pattern — A normal bowel gas pattern does not show multiple dilated loops with fluid levels.
• Chronic constipation only — Constipation shows faecal loading, not multiple air–fluid levels.
• Gallstones — Gallstones are often not visible on X-ray (only about 10–15% contain calcium); they don't cause this pattern.

⭐ Key point: Signs of obstruction: colicky pain, vomiting, distension, absolute constipation. Treatment is "drip and suck": IV fluids plus NG tube on free drainage, nil by mouth.$x$),
(44, $x$Which nursing intervention is most effective in preventing contrast-induced nephropathy in an at-risk patient before a CT scan with IV contrast?$x$, $x$Ensuring adequate IV isotonic fluid hydration before and after the procedure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ensuring adequate IV isotonic fluid hydration before and after the procedure
Contrast can damage the kidney tubules and narrow the kidney blood vessels, causing a rise in creatinine 24–72 hours later. The most effective preventive step is good IV HYDRATION with isotonic fluid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's) before and after the scan. This keeps urine flowing, dilutes the contrast and protects the kidneys. Also use the lowest dose of low-osmolar contrast, stop nephrotoxic drugs (NSAIDs), and hold metformin if the GFR is low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administering prophylactic antibiotics — Antibiotics have no role in preventing contrast kidney injury.
• Withholding all oral fluids for 12 hours before the scan — Withholding fluids causes dehydration, which INCREASES the risk.
• Administering a loop diuretic prior to the scan — Loop diuretics can dehydrate the patient and increase the risk.

⭐ Key point: High-risk patients are those with CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure and older age. Check creatinine at 48–72 hours after contrast.$x$),
(45, $x$Which chest X-ray finding is characteristic of cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?$x$, $x$Bilateral perihilar 'bat-wing' infiltrates with Kerley B lines and cardiomegaly$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Bilateral perihilar 'bat-wing' infiltrates with Kerley B lines and cardiomegaly
In cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, the failing left ventricle backs up pressure into the lungs. On X-ray you see: an enlarged heart (cardiomegaly), fluid around the central airways in a "bat-wing" (butterfly) pattern, Kerley B lines (short horizontal lines at the lung edges, from swollen lymphatics), upper-lobe vessel diversion, and often pleural effusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single well-circumscribed nodule in the right upper lobe — A single nodule suggests a tumour or granuloma, not pulmonary oedema.
• Complete opacification of one hemithorax with mediastinal shift away from it — Complete whiteness of one side with shift AWAY from it suggests a massive pleural effusion.
• A normal chest X-ray in all cases — The X-ray is usually abnormal in pulmonary oedema, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary oedema care: sit the patient upright, oxygen or CPAP, GTN (if BP allows), and IV furosemide. In ARDS, the heart size is usually normal.$x$),
(46, $x$A Processor acts like a/an$x$, $x$BRAIN$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: BRAIN
The processor (CPU, central processing unit) is the "brain" of the computer. It carries out instructions, does calculations and controls all other parts, just as the brain controls the body. Its speed is measured in gigahertz (GHz).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HEART — The heart pumps blood; in a computer, the power supply is a better comparison.
• ARM — An arm is like an output device (it carries out actions), not the processor.
• KIDNEY — The kidney filters waste; there is no CPU comparison with the kidney.

⭐ Key point: CPU = brain, RAM = short-term memory (working desk), hard disk = long-term storage (filing cabinet), and input/output devices = the senses and hands.$x$),
(47, $x$What is the main function of RAM (Random Access Memory) in a computer?$x$, $x$To temporarily store data that the CPU is actively using$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To temporarily store data that the CPU is actively using
RAM (random access memory) is the computer's TEMPORARY working memory. It holds the programs and data the CPU is using right now, so the computer can work quickly. RAM is volatile: everything in it is lost when the power is switched off. More RAM lets more programs run smoothly at the same time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To store data permanently — Permanent storage is the job of the hard disk, SSD or flash drive.
• To store the operating system — The operating system is stored permanently on the hard disk; it is only loaded into RAM while running.
• To provide an internet connection — Internet connection comes from the network card or modem, not RAM.

⭐ Key point: RAM is temporary and erased when off. ROM (read-only memory) is permanent and holds the start-up instructions.$x$),
(48, $x$What is the purpose of an Electronic Health Record (EHR)?$x$, $x$To create a digital version of a patient's chart$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To create a digital version of a patient's chart
An electronic health record (EHR) is a digital (computer) version of the patient's chart. It holds the full health information — history, diagnoses, drugs, allergies, lab results, imaging, vital signs, nursing notes and care plans — and is available to authorised staff wherever needed. It improves legibility, speeds sharing of information, supports alerts (allergies, drug interactions) and reduces errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To store only the patient's demographic information — An EHR stores the FULL record, not only demographic details.
• To serve as a paper-based filing system — An EHR replaces paper files; it is not a paper system.
• To replace the need for any patient-nurse interaction — An EHR supports care; it never replaces the nurse–patient relationship.

⭐ Key point: EHRs must be protected: use your own login, never share passwords, log out after use, and respect confidentiality.$x$),
(49, $x$Telemedicine refers to:$x$, $x$The use of telecommunications technology to deliver healthcare services remotely$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The use of telecommunications technology to deliver healthcare services remotely
Telemedicine means using telecommunication technology — phone, video calls, internet and data links — to give healthcare at a DISTANCE: consultations, monitoring, diagnosis and education. In critical care, "tele-ICU" allows specialists to support teams in remote hospitals and review monitors and images from afar. It improves access in rural areas and saves travel time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A type of medical imaging equipment — Imaging equipment (X-ray, CT) is a diagnostic device, not telemedicine itself.
• A brand of hospital software only used for billing — Billing software is administrative and is not what telemedicine means.
• A paper-based referral system — A paper referral system does not use telecommunication.

⭐ Key point: Telemedicine needs patient consent, privacy and secure systems, the same as face-to-face care.$x$),
(50, $x$A cardiac monitor and pulse oximeter used in the ICU are examples of which category of electronic devices in healthcare?$x$, $x$Monitoring devices$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Monitoring devices
A cardiac monitor and a pulse oximeter continuously WATCH the patient's vital signs (heart rhythm, heart rate, oxygen saturation) and raise alarms when values go outside the set limits. That makes them monitoring devices. They do not treat the patient or make the diagnosis on their own; they alert the nurse to changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnostic devices — Diagnostic devices are used mainly to find a diagnosis, such as X-ray machines, CT scanners and lab analysers.
• Treatment devices — Treatment (therapeutic) devices deliver therapy, such as ventilators, infusion pumps and defibrillators.
• Storage devices — Storage devices hold data, such as hard drives and USB drives.

⭐ Key point: Set alarm limits for each patient, never switch alarms off, and always check the PATIENT, not just the monitor.$x$),
(51, $x$A classic clinical feature of acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema is:$x$, $x$Pink, frothy sputum with severe dyspnoea and orthopnoea$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pink, frothy sputum with severe dyspnoea and orthopnoea
In acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, the left ventricle fails and pressure backs up into the lung capillaries. Fluid leaks into the alveoli and mixes with air, producing PINK (blood-stained), FROTHY sputum. The patient is severely breathless, cannot lie flat (orthopnoea), is sweaty and anxious, and has widespread crackles, often with wheeze ("cardiac asthma").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless haematuria — Painless haematuria is a urinary sign (kidney or bladder tumour, stones), not pulmonary oedema.
• Unilateral leg swelling with a normal respiratory rate — One swollen leg with normal breathing suggests a DVT, not pulmonary oedema.
• Dry cough with no dyspnoea — A dry cough without breathlessness does not match the severe distress of acute pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Treat by sitting the patient upright with legs down, giving oxygen or CPAP, GTN (if SBP is above 90–100) and IV furosemide, and monitoring urine output.$x$),
(52, $x$Drug therapy for pulmonary oedema includes the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Haematinics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Haematinics
The question asks which drug is NOT used. Haematinics (iron, folic acid, vitamin B12) treat anaemia; they do nothing for fluid in the lungs. All the other options can be used: diuretics remove fluid, morphine reduces anxiety and preload, and digitalis strengthens contraction and controls AF rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretics — Diuretics (furosemide) ARE used: they remove excess fluid and quickly dilate the veins.
• Morphine — Morphine IS used (now more carefully): it eases breathlessness and anxiety and lowers preload.
• Digitalis — Digitalis (digoxin) IS used in some patients, especially with AF, to slow the rate and improve pumping.

⭐ Key point: Remember "LMNOP" for pulmonary oedema: Lasix (furosemide), Morphine (with caution), Nitrates, Oxygen, Position (sit up).$x$),
(53, $x$A patient develops sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic chest pain, and tachycardia after prolonged bed rest post-surgery. The nurse should have a high index of suspicion for:$x$, $x$Pulmonary embolism$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
After surgery and bed rest, blood flows slowly in the leg veins (stasis), surgery makes the blood clot more easily, and veins may be damaged. These three factors (Virchow's triad) cause a DVT. A piece of clot can travel to the lungs as a pulmonary embolism, causing SUDDEN breathlessness, sharp chest pain worse on breathing in (pleuritic), fast pulse, low oxygen, and sometimes haemoptysis or collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety should never be assumed until serious causes like PE are excluded.
• Common cold — A common cold causes runny nose and sore throat, not sudden pleuritic pain.
• Gastritis — Gastritis causes epigastric burning, not sudden breathlessness with tachycardia.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PE with early mobilisation, leg exercises, compression stockings and LMWH. Suspect PE with any sudden breathlessness after surgery.$x$),
(54, $x$CT pulmonary angiography is the imaging investigation of choice to diagnose:$x$, $x$Pulmonary embolism$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) injects contrast into a vein and scans the chest as the contrast passes through the lung arteries. A clot shows as a dark "filling defect" in a bright vessel. It is fast, widely available and accurate, so it is the first-choice test for PE. A V/Q scan is used when contrast cannot be given (kidney failure, contrast allergy, pregnancy in some centres).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A rib fracture is seen on a plain X-ray or a normal CT; contrast angiography is not needed.
• Peptic ulcer disease — Peptic ulcer is diagnosed by endoscopy (OGD).
• Osteoarthritis — Osteoarthritis is diagnosed clinically and with plain X-rays.

⭐ Key point: Use the Wells score and D-dimer first in stable patients. A low score with a normal D-dimer makes PE unlikely.$x$),
(55, $x$A patient with severe emphysema is classically described as a 'pink puffer.' This term reflects:$x$, $x$A thin body habitus, pursed-lip breathing, and relatively preserved oxygenation until late disease$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A thin body habitus, pursed-lip breathing, and relatively preserved oxygenation until late disease
In emphysema, the alveolar walls are destroyed and the lungs over-inflate. The patient works very hard to breathe: they are thin (they burn many calories breathing), barrel-chested, use accessory muscles, and breathe out through pursed lips to keep the airways open. Because they breathe so hard, oxygen levels stay fairly normal until late, so they look "pink" and "puff". This is the opposite of the "blue bloater".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cyanosis with peripheral oedema — Cyanosis with peripheral oedema describes the "blue bloater" (chronic bronchitis with right heart failure).
• Obesity with cor pulmonale — Obesity with cor pulmonale also fits the blue bloater, not the pink puffer.
• A productive cough with copious purulent sputum — A productive cough with lots of sputum is typical of chronic bronchitis (blue bloater).

⭐ Key point: Pink puffer = emphysema (thin, breathless, near-normal O₂). Blue bloater = chronic bronchitis (overweight, cyanosed, oedema, CO₂ retention).$x$),
(56, $x$For a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation and a history of chronic CO2 retention, which initial oxygen delivery approach is most appropriate?$x$, $x$Controlled oxygen via Venturi mask titrated to a target SpO2 of about 88-92%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Controlled oxygen via Venturi mask titrated to a target SpO2 of about 88-92%
Patients with COPD who retain CO₂ may depend partly on low oxygen to drive their breathing. Too much oxygen can raise CO₂ further (through less breathing drive, V/Q mismatch and the Haldane effect), causing CO₂ narcosis, acidosis and drowsiness. A Venturi mask gives an EXACT, fixed oxygen percentage (24% or 28% to start), titrated to a target SpO₂ of 88–92%. Check an ABG within 30–60 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min regardless of saturation — A non-rebreather at 15 L/min gives very high oxygen and risks CO₂ narcosis; it is for other emergencies.
• Room air only with no supplemental oxygen under any circumstance — Withholding oxygen completely is dangerous; hypoxia kills faster than CO₂ retention.
• Nasal cannula at 6 L/min titrated to SpO2 100% — Aiming for 100% saturation in a CO₂ retainer causes oxygen-induced hypercapnia.

⭐ Key point: COPD target SpO₂ is 88–92%. If pH is below 7.35 with a high PaCO₂ despite treatment, start BiPAP (NIV).$x$),
(57, $x$How is a tension pneumothorax causing PEA arrest managed?$x$, $x$Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion
Tension pneumothorax causes PEA by trapping air that crushes the lung and pushes the heart and great veins aside, so blood cannot return to the heart. It is one of the reversible "4 Ts". The cause must be relieved IMMEDIATELY with needle decompression (2nd ICS mid-clavicular line, or 4th–5th ICS mid-axillary line in adults), followed by a chest drain. Continue CPR while this is done.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Defibrillation — Defibrillation does not work for PEA; it is only for VF and pulseless VT.
• IV fluids alone — IV fluids alone cannot overcome the blockage to blood return.
• Atropine administration — Atropine is no longer recommended for PEA and does not treat the cause.

⭐ Key point: The reversible causes of cardiac arrest are the 4 Hs (Hypoxia, Hypovolaemia, Hypo/Hyperkalaemia, Hypothermia) and the 4 Ts (Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis).$x$),
(58, $x$Which site is commonly used for chest tube insertion?$x$, $x$5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line
The usual chest drain site is the "safe triangle": 4th–5th intercostal space in the MID-AXILLARY line (lateral edge of pectoralis major in front, lateral edge of latissimus dorsi behind, the level of the nipple below). This avoids the internal mammary artery, the heart, the liver and spleen, and large muscles. The drain goes over the TOP of the rib, because the intercostal vessels and nerve run along the bottom edge.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midline of the abdomen — The midline of the abdomen is for abdominal procedures (such as a laparotomy), not chest drains.
• Clavicular notch — The clavicular notch is not a chest drain site; it is near large vessels.
• 4th intercostal space, midclavicular line — The 4th ICS mid-clavicular line is too close to the heart and breast tissue; the 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line is used for NEEDLE decompression.

⭐ Key point: The patient should sit up with the arm raised behind the head. After insertion, check for swinging and bubbling and get a chest X-ray.$x$),
(59, $x$Type II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure is defined by:$x$, $x$A PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with accompanying respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with accompanying respiratory acidosis
Type II (hypercapnic or ventilatory) respiratory failure means the lungs cannot remove CO₂: PaCO₂ above 45–50 mmHg (above 6 kPa), usually with a low PaO₂ and a low pH (respiratory acidosis) when it is acute. The cause is HYPOventilation: COPD, exhausted asthma, opioid overdose, neuromuscular disease (GBS, myasthenia), obesity and chest wall problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A PaO2 less than 60 mmHg alone — A PaO₂ below 60 mmHg alone (normal or low CO₂) is TYPE I (hypoxaemic) failure.
• A normal PaCO2 with severe hypoxemia — A normal PaCO₂ with severe hypoxaemia is also type I failure.
• Elevated bicarbonate with normal pH — A raised bicarbonate with normal pH is a compensated or metabolic picture, not the definition.

⭐ Key point: Type 1 = low O₂ only (oxygenation problem: pneumonia, oedema, ARDS). Type 2 = low O₂ + HIGH CO₂ (ventilation problem). Type 2 needs ventilation support (NIV or intubation), not just oxygen.$x$),
(60, $x$A key clinical benefit of BiPAP in a patient with a COPD exacerbation and respiratory failure is that it:$x$, $x$Reduces the work of breathing by providing inspiratory pressure support while maintaining alveolar recruitment with EPAP$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reduces the work of breathing by providing inspiratory pressure support while maintaining alveolar recruitment with EPAP
BiPAP gives two pressures. IPAP (inspiratory pressure) pushes air in on each breath, helping the tired breathing muscles, increasing tidal volume and blowing off CO₂. EPAP (expiratory pressure, which acts like PEEP) keeps the small airways and alveoli open at the end of each breath, so less work is needed for the next breath. In COPD exacerbations it lowers CO₂, corrects the pH and reduces the need for intubation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Eliminates the need for any pharmacologic bronchodilator therapy — BiPAP does not replace bronchodilators; nebulisers, steroids and antibiotics are still needed.
• Cures the underlying airway obstruction — BiPAP supports breathing while the exacerbation is treated; it does not cure the disease.
• Requires no patient cooperation or spontaneous breathing effort — BiPAP needs a cooperative, spontaneously breathing patient who can protect their airway.

⭐ Key point: Start NIV in COPD when pH is 7.25–7.35 with a high PaCO₂. Check the ABG at 1 hour; no improvement means the patient may need intubation.$x$),
(61, $x$What PaO2/FiO2 ratio threshold, with PEEP of at least 5 cmH2O, defines severe ARDS under the Berlin definition?$x$, $x$100 or less$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 100 or less
The Berlin definition grades ARDS by the PaO₂/FiO₂ (P/F) ratio, with PEEP or CPAP of at least 5 cmH₂O. SEVERE is P/F of 100 or less, moderate is above 100 to 200, and mild is above 200 to 300. Example: PaO₂ 70 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.8 gives 70 ÷ 0.8 = 87.5, which is severe ARDS. Severe ARDS has the highest death rate and needs prone positioning and sometimes ECMO.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 300 or less — 300 or less is the upper cut-off for MILD ARDS (the threshold for any ARDS).
• 200 or less — 200 or less is the cut-off for MODERATE ARDS.
• 150 or less — 150 or less is the cut-off used to start PRONE positioning, not the Berlin severe category.

⭐ Key point: P/F ratio = PaO₂ (mmHg) ÷ FiO₂ (as a decimal). Normal is about 400–500 (100 ÷ 0.21 ≈ 476).$x$),
(62, $x$What is the normal recommended tidal volume range for lung-protective ventilation in ARDS, based on predicted body weight?$x$, $x$6-8 mL/kg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6-8 mL/kg
Lung-protective ventilation uses SMALL tidal volumes of 6–8 mL/kg PREDICTED body weight (starting at 6 mL/kg in ARDS), with a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O or less. ARDS lungs are "baby lungs": only a small part works, so large breaths over-stretch and damage it (volutrauma). The ARDSNet trial showed that 6 mL/kg instead of 12 mL/kg reduced deaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10-12 mL/kg — 10–12 mL/kg was the old practice and increases lung injury and death.
• 15-18 mL/kg — 15–18 mL/kg is dangerously large and would cause barotrauma and volutrauma.
• 2-4 mL/kg — 2–4 mL/kg is too small for normal ventilation (only used in special settings like ECMO).

⭐ Key point: Use PREDICTED body weight (from height and sex), not actual weight. An obese patient's lungs are no bigger than a thin patient's of the same height.$x$),
(63, $x$A patient's serum sodium is measured at 118mmol/L with confusion and lethargy. The nurse understands that correction of severe hyponatraemia must be done:$x$, $x$Gradually and carefully, since overly rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Gradually and carefully, since overly rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination syndrome
In chronic low sodium, brain cells adapt by losing solutes to avoid swelling. If sodium is raised too FAST, water rushes out of the brain cells and damages the myelin in the pons, causing osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis). This leads to difficulty speaking and swallowing, quadriplegia, "locked-in" syndrome or death. Correction must therefore be gradual: no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours, with frequent sodium checks (every 2–4 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• As rapidly as possible regardless of rate — Correcting as fast as possible is exactly what causes osmotic demyelination.
• Only with oral salt tablets, never IV — IV treatment (including 3% saline for severe symptoms such as seizures) is used when needed; oral salt alone is not the rule.
• With no monitoring required — Close monitoring of sodium, fluid balance and neurological status is essential.

⭐ Key point: For severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (seizures, coma), give 3% saline 150 mL boluses to raise sodium by 4–6 mmol/L quickly, then slow down to stay within 8–10 mmol/L per 24 hours.$x$),
(64, $x$Which ventricle is most sensitive to an increase in afterload?$x$, $x$A failing ventricle with poor contractility$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A failing ventricle with poor contractility
Afterload is the resistance the ventricle must pump against. A healthy heart can keep its stroke volume even when afterload rises. A FAILING ventricle with weak contractility cannot overcome extra resistance, so its stroke volume falls sharply when afterload rises. That is why lowering afterload (ACE inhibitors, nitrates, nitroprusside, IABP) improves output in heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal ventricle — A normal ventricle can compensate well, so its stroke volume changes little.
• A hypertrophied athletic ventricle — A hypertrophied athletic heart is strong and handles extra afterload well.
• None, afterload never affects stroke volume — Afterload DOES affect stroke volume; it matters most in the failing heart.

⭐ Key point: In heart failure, reduce afterload with vasodilators and avoid high blood pressure. In septic shock, afterload (SVR) is LOW and vasopressors raise it.$x$),
(65, $x$A patient reports substernal chest pressure radiating to the jaw during exertion, relieved within 5 minutes of rest. The nurse should classify this as most consistent with:$x$, $x$Stable angina$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stable angina
STABLE angina is chest pain brought on by a predictable amount of exertion (walking, climbing stairs) or emotion. It lasts a few minutes (usually under 10–15 minutes) and goes away with REST or GTN. It is caused by a fixed narrowing of the coronary artery. Here, the pain comes on with exertion and is relieved within 5 minutes of rest, which is the classic stable pattern.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unstable angina — Unstable angina is pain at rest, new pain, or pain that is getting worse (crescendo); it is an emergency (ACS).
• Acute MI — An acute MI causes pain lasting more than 20 minutes, not relieved by rest, with ECG changes and raised troponin.
• Pericarditis — Pericarditis causes sharp pain, worse lying down and on breathing, better sitting forward, not related to exertion.

⭐ Key point: Any change in the pattern of angina (at rest, more often, longer, less relief) is unstable angina. Treat it as ACS.$x$),
(66, $x$Which cardiac biomarker is most specific for myocardial injury and remains elevated for 7-14 days after MI?$x$, $x$Troponin I/T$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Troponin I/T
Troponin I and T are proteins found only in heart muscle, so they are the most SPECIFIC markers of heart muscle damage. Levels rise 3–6 hours after the injury, peak at 12–24 hours, and stay raised for 7–14 days. This makes troponin useful to diagnose MI even if the patient comes late. High-sensitivity troponin tests at 0 and 1–3 hours are now standard.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CK-MB — CK-MB is less specific (some is in skeletal muscle) and returns to normal in 2–3 days, so it is used to detect REINFARCTION.
• Myoglobin — Myoglobin rises earliest (1–2 hours) but is found in all muscle, so it is not specific.
• LDH — LDH rises late and is non-specific (blood, liver, muscle); it is no longer used for MI.

⭐ Key point: Troponin can also rise in sepsis, PE, kidney failure, myocarditis and after CPR or defibrillation, so interpret it with the ECG and symptoms.$x$),
(67, $x$A patient post-myocardial infarction develops new-onset crackles bilaterally, a third heart sound (S3), and worsening dyspnoea. This most likely indicates:$x$, $x$Acute left ventricular failure/pulmonary oedema$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute left ventricular failure/pulmonary oedema
After an MI, the damaged left ventricle may fail to pump forward. Blood backs up into the lungs (pulmonary oedema), causing new bilateral crackles and worsening breathlessness. An S3 (third heart sound, "Ken-tuc-ky") comes from blood rushing into a stiff, overfilled ventricle, a classic sign of LV failure. This is acute left ventricular failure (Killip class II–III).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety does not produce crackles and an S3.
• Normal post-MI recovery — These are signs of a complication, not normal recovery.
• Gastro-oesophageal reflux — Reflux causes heartburn, not crackles and an S3.

⭐ Key point: Post-MI complications include arrhythmias, heart failure and cardiogenic shock, papillary muscle rupture (a new murmur), ventricular rupture, and pericarditis (including Dressler's syndrome).$x$),
(68, $x$The most common cause of right-sided heart failure is:$x$, $x$Chronic left-sided heart failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic left-sided heart failure
The commonest cause of right heart failure is LEFT heart failure. When the left side fails, pressure backs up into the lungs (pulmonary hypertension). The right ventricle then has to pump against this high pressure and eventually fails too. Signs of right heart failure: raised JVP, swollen ankles (pitting oedema), enlarged liver, ascites.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aortic stenosis — Aortic stenosis affects the left ventricle first; it can lead to right failure, but it is not the commonest cause.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism can cause high-output failure but is not the commonest cause of right failure.
• Anemia — Anaemia can cause high-output failure but is uncommon as a cause of right failure.

⭐ Key point: Right heart failure caused by LUNG disease (COPD, PE, pulmonary hypertension) is called cor pulmonale.$x$),
(69, $x$One of these is an indication for a permanent pacemaker$x$, $x$Complete heart block$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Complete heart block
Complete (third-degree) heart block means NO impulses pass from the atria to the ventricles. The ventricles beat on their own slow escape rhythm (often 30–40/min), causing dizziness, fainting (Stokes–Adams attacks), heart failure or sudden death. Because it rarely recovers (except after an inferior MI or from drugs), a PERMANENT pacemaker is the definitive treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachyarrthymias — Tachyarrhythmias are usually treated with drugs, ablation or an ICD, not a standard pacemaker.
• Carotid sinus syndrome — Carotid sinus syndrome can need a pacemaker in severe cases, but complete heart block is the classic, definite indication.
• Myocardial infarction — An MI alone is not an indication; temporary pacing may be needed for some blocks after MI.

⭐ Key point: Indications for a permanent pacemaker: complete heart block, Mobitz II block, symptomatic sick sinus syndrome, and symptomatic bradycardia without a reversible cause.$x$),
(70, $x$a 62 years old male patient has an ECG done that shows PR interval to be fixed, but drop QRS randomly. It indiciates?$x$, $x$second degree type-II block$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: second degree type-II block
Second-degree TYPE II (Mobitz II) heart block: the PR interval stays the SAME (fixed) for each conducted beat, but suddenly a P wave is NOT followed by a QRS (a dropped beat), without warning. The block is usually below the AV node (in the His–Purkinje system), so it can suddenly progress to complete heart block. It needs pacing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• first degree block — First-degree block has a long PR (above 0.20 s) but NO dropped beats.
• second degree type-I block — Second-degree type I (Wenckebach) has a PR that gets LONGER and LONGER until a beat drops, then the cycle repeats.
• third degree block — Third-degree block has NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes (they march independently).

⭐ Key point: Wenckebach = "longer, longer, longer, drop" (usually benign). Mobitz II = "fixed PR, sudden drop" (dangerous; prepare for pacing).$x$),
(71, $x$A patient with a permanent pacemaker shows a rhythm strip with pacer spikes not followed by a QRS complex. This indicates:$x$, $x$Failure to capture — the pacemaker impulse is not depolarising the myocardium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Failure to capture — the pacemaker impulse is not depolarising the myocardium
Each pacing spike should be immediately followed by a QRS complex (ventricular pacing) or a P wave (atrial pacing). This means the impulse has "captured" the heart. A spike with NO QRS after it is FAILURE TO CAPTURE: the electrical output is not making the heart muscle contract. Causes: lead displacement, a lead fracture, energy output too low, scar tissue at the lead tip, low battery, high potassium, or drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal pacemaker function — Normal function means every spike is followed by a complex.
• Battery is fully charged with no issue — A battery problem is one possible CAUSE of failure to capture; the strip does not show a healthy battery.
• Over-sensing with no clinical relevance — Over-sensing means the pacemaker detects signals it shouldn't and fails to pace (no spikes when needed); it is clinically important.

⭐ Key point: Pacemaker problems: failure to PACE (no spike), failure to CAPTURE (spike with no complex), and failure to SENSE (spikes in the wrong place). Check the patient's pulse and BP first, then call for help.$x$),
(72, $x$It is a flexible catheter, inserted into the femoral artery and passed into the descending aorta 1-2 cm below the left subclavian artery and slightly above the renal arteries.$x$, $x$Intra-aortic balloon pump$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intra-aortic balloon pump
The intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) is a long balloon catheter inserted through the femoral artery into the descending thoracic aorta. The tip sits just below the left subclavian artery, and the balloon ends above the renal arteries. It INFLATES in diastole (pushing blood back into the coronary arteries, improving heart muscle oxygen) and DEFLATES just before systole (lowering afterload so the heart pumps more easily). It is used in cardiogenic shock and severe ischaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary Artery Wedge Pressure — PAWP is a pressure measurement, not a device.
• Pulmonary Artery Catheter — A pulmonary artery catheter goes through a vein into the right heart and pulmonary artery, not into the aorta.
• None of the above — One of the options (IABP) does fit the description, so "none" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: IABP nursing: check leg pulses, colour and warmth (limb ischaemia); check the LEFT radial pulse (the tip may block the left subclavian); check urine output (it may block the renal arteries); keep the leg straight; watch for bleeding.$x$),
(73, $x$Which one of the following is NOT a function of pulmonary arterial catheter (PAC) which is also called Swan GANZ catheter?$x$, $x$Measurement of right atrium pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Measurement of right atrium pressure
The pulmonary artery catheter is placed mainly to measure pressures in the pulmonary artery, the wedge pressure (which estimates left ventricular filling) and cardiac output. The question's answer key marks "measurement of right atrium pressure" as NOT its function. The reason: right atrial pressure (CVP) is usually measured with an ordinary central venous catheter, and measuring it is not the reason for placing a Swan-Ganz. (Strictly, the PAC's proximal port can show RA pressure too, so read questions like this carefully.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous cardiac output monitoring — Continuous cardiac output monitoring IS a PAC function (thermodilution).
• Measurement of pulmonary artery pressure — Measuring pulmonary artery pressure IS the main PAC function.
• Estimation of diastole filling of left ventricle — Estimating left ventricular diastolic filling IS a PAC function, through the wedge pressure (PAWP).

⭐ Key point: PAC measures PA pressure (15–25/8–15), PAWP (6–12, left heart filling), cardiac output (4–8 L/min) and SvO₂ (60–80%).$x$),
(74, $x$The first-line vasopressor of choice in septic shock is:$x$, $x$Norepinephrine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Norepinephrine
Noradrenaline (norepinephrine) is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock (Surviving Sepsis Campaign). Septic shock is a VASODILATED shock (low SVR). Noradrenaline mainly stimulates alpha-1 receptors, tightening blood vessels and raising MAP, with less effect on heart rate. The target is MAP of 65 mmHg or more after fluids. Ideally it runs through a central line, but it can be started peripherally in a big vein for a short time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dopamine — Dopamine causes more arrhythmias and higher death rates than noradrenaline; it is no longer first-line.
• Adrenaline — Adrenaline is added as a second or third agent (or for low cardiac output), not first-line.
• Vasopressin — Vasopressin is ADDED to noradrenaline (0.03 units/min) to reduce the noradrenaline dose, not used first.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis Hour-1 bundle: lactate, blood cultures, antibiotics, 30 mL/kg crystalloid for low BP, and noradrenaline for MAP below 65.$x$),
(75, $x$A trauma patient in hypovolaemic shock has lost an estimated 35% of their circulating blood volume. According to classification of haemorrhagic shock, this places them in:$x$, $x$Class III$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Class III
Blood loss classes (ATLS): Class I up to 15%, Class II 15–30%, Class III 30–40%, Class IV over 40%. A loss of 35% falls into CLASS III (about 1500–2000 mL in a 70 kg adult). In Class III, the pulse is above 120, systolic BP FALLS, breathing is fast, urine output is low (5–15 mL/h) and the patient is anxious or confused. They need blood transfusion, not just crystalloid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Class I — Class I (under 15%) has almost normal vital signs.
• Class II — Class II (15–30%) has tachycardia and a narrow pulse pressure, but the BP is usually still normal.
• Class IV — Class IV (over 40%) is immediately life-threatening, with very low BP, lethargy and almost no urine.

⭐ Key point: A falling BP is a LATE sign of blood loss (Class III). Tachycardia, narrowed pulse pressure, cool skin and slow capillary refill come first.$x$),
(76, $x$Early (warm) septic shock differs from hypovolaemic shock by all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Tachycardia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia
The question asks which feature does NOT differ. Both early (warm) septic shock and hypovolaemic shock cause TACHYCARDIA, so it does not help tell them apart. What differs is the skin: in warm sepsis, the blood vessels are dilated, so the skin is warm, flushed and well-perfused. In hypovolaemic shock, the vessels are clamped down, so the skin is cold, pale and clammy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm extremities — Warm extremities ARE different: warm in sepsis, cold in hypovolaemia.
• Flushed skin — Flushed skin IS different: flushed in sepsis, pale in hypovolaemia.
• Well-perfused extremities — Well-perfused extremities ARE different (sepsis) from poorly perfused ones (hypovolaemia).

⭐ Key point: Warm shock (low SVR) = septic, anaphylactic and neurogenic. Cold shock (high SVR) = hypovolaemic, cardiogenic and obstructive. Late septic shock can also become cold.$x$),
(77, $x$What is a common cause of acute kidney injury (AKI) in the ICU?$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
All three are common causes of AKI in ICU, and often several act together. Sepsis is the commonest cause: it causes low BP, inflammation and microcirculation damage. Dehydration or hypovolaemia (bleeding, vomiting, diuretics) reduces kidney blood flow (pre-renal AKI). Nephrotoxic drugs damage the tubules: aminoglycosides (gentamicin), vancomycin, NSAIDs, IV contrast, amphotericin and ACE inhibitors in dehydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sepsis — Sepsis alone is a cause, but not the only one listed.
• Dehydration — Dehydration alone is a cause, but not the only one listed.
• Nephrotoxic drugs — Nephrotoxic drugs alone are a cause, but not the only one listed.

⭐ Key point: Prevent AKI: keep MAP of 65 or more, keep the patient well hydrated, avoid or monitor nephrotoxic drugs (check trough levels), and watch urine output hourly (aim for at least 0.5 mL/kg/h).$x$),
(78, $x$Kidney failure is a potential postoperative complication in the ICU. Which laboratory test would be most indicative of kidney function?$x$, $x$Creatinine levels$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Creatinine levels
Creatinine is a waste product of muscle that the kidneys filter at a steady rate. When kidney function falls, creatinine RISES in the blood. It is the standard simple test of kidney function and is used to estimate GFR and to diagnose AKI (KDIGO: a rise of 26.5 µmol/L or more within 48 hours, or 1.5 times baseline, or urine below 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucose levels — Glucose measures diabetes control, not kidney function.
• Potassium levels — Potassium often rises in kidney failure and is important to monitor, but it is not the best measure of kidney function itself.
• Sodium levels — Sodium reflects water balance, not kidney filtration.

⭐ Key point: Creatinine rises LATE (24–48 hours after injury). Falling urine output is often the EARLIEST sign of AKI, so measure it hourly.$x$),
(79, $x$A patient with hyperkalemia has peaked T waves. After calcium gluconate, which drug shifts potassium intracellularly?$x$, $x$Albuterol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Albuterol
After calcium gluconate protects the heart, the next step is to SHIFT potassium from the blood into the cells. Albuterol (salbutamol), a beta-2 agonist, does this by stimulating the sodium-potassium pump, lowering K⁺ by about 0.5–1 mmol/L within 30 minutes. It is given by nebuliser (10–20 mg). Insulin with glucose works the same way and is the most reliable shifting drug.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sodium polystyrene — Sodium polystyrene (Kayexalate/calcium resonium) REMOVES potassium through the gut, slowly (hours); it doesn't shift it.
• Furosemide — Furosemide REMOVES potassium in the urine; it doesn't shift it into cells.
• Fludrocortisone — Fludrocortisone increases potassium loss over days; it is not used acutely.

⭐ Key point: Order for high potassium: 1) PROTECT the heart (calcium gluconate 10%), 2) SHIFT K⁺ into cells (insulin and glucose, salbutamol), 3) REMOVE K⁺ (dialysis, resins, diuretics).$x$),
(80, $x$In Arteriovenous fistula:$x$, $x$There is mixture of arterial and venous blood$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: There is mixture of arterial and venous blood
An arteriovenous (AV) fistula is a surgical joining of an ARTERY directly to a VEIN, usually in the forearm (radial artery to cephalic vein). Arterial blood at high pressure flows straight into the vein, so the vein contains a mixture of arterial and venous blood. The vein enlarges and its wall thickens ("matures" over 6–12 weeks), so it can be needled for haemodialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is mixture of arterial blood — It is not only arterial blood; the blood goes into a vein.
• There is mixture of venous blood — It is not only venous blood; arterial blood enters the vein directly.
• There is mixture of artery and capillary blood — Capillaries are bypassed in a fistula; there is no artery–capillary mixing.

⭐ Key point: Fistula care: feel for the THRILL (buzz) and listen for the BRUIT (whoosh) daily. Never take BP, put a cannula in, or take blood from the fistula arm.$x$),
(81, $x$A hemodialysis patient develops sudden hypotension, nausea, headache, and confusion approximately 3 hours into a first dialysis session. This presentation is most consistent with:$x$, $x$Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome
Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome happens during or just after the FIRST dialysis sessions, especially when urea is very high and is removed too quickly. Urea falls fast in the blood but slowly in the brain, so water moves INTO brain cells (cerebral oedema). Signs: headache, nausea, vomiting, restlessness, confusion, hypotension, seizures, even coma. Prevent it with short, slow first sessions (low blood flow) and a gradual urea reduction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaphylaxis to the dialyzer membrane — Dialyser anaphylaxis happens within MINUTES of starting, with wheeze, urticaria and itching.
• Air embolism — Air embolism causes sudden breathlessness, chest pain and collapse, not slowly developing headache and confusion.
• Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest — Hyperkalaemia improves during dialysis (K⁺ is removed), so cardiac arrest from high K⁺ is unlikely 3 hours in.

⭐ Key point: Treat by slowing or stopping dialysis and giving osmotic agents (mannitol or hypertonic saline) if severe. Monitor neuro status during first sessions.$x$),
(82, $x$Hypothermia is a common complication of CRRT because:$x$, $x$Extracorporeal blood is exposed to room-temperature tubing, filters, and replacement fluids, causing heat loss$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Extracorporeal blood is exposed to room-temperature tubing, filters, and replacement fluids, causing heat loss
In CRRT, blood leaves the body and runs through long tubing and a filter at room temperature, and large volumes of replacement fluid or dialysate (often several litres per hour) are added. The blood loses heat outside the body and on mixing with cooler fluid, so the patient's temperature falls. Hypothermia can also HIDE a fever, so infection may be missed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CRRT machines actively cool the blood intentionally at all times — CRRT machines do not deliberately cool the blood.
• Patients are typically febrile at baseline — Patients may or may not have fever; this does not explain the cooling.
• The dialysate is always heated above body temperature — Dialysate is warmed TO body temperature (not above it) to reduce heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Prevent CRRT hypothermia with the fluid warmer and blankets or forced-air warmers. Monitor temperature hourly, and remember a normal temperature on CRRT does not rule out infection.$x$),
(83, $x$A patient opens their eyes only to painful stimuli, makes incomprehensible sounds, and withdraws from pain. What is the calculated GCS score?$x$, $x$8$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8
Add up the three parts of the GCS. EYES open only to pain = E2. VERBAL: incomprehensible sounds (moaning, groaning, no words) = V2. MOTOR: withdraws from pain (pulls the limb away) = M4. Total = 2 + 2 + 4 = 8. A GCS of 8 is a severe brain injury, and the airway may need protection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 — 6 would mean a lower motor or verbal score (for example abnormal flexion, M3).
• 10 — 10 would need higher scores, such as confused words (V4) or localising pain (M5).
• 12 — 12 would need much better scores, such as eyes opening to voice and localising pain.

⭐ Key point: GCS: Eyes 1–4, Verbal 1–5, Motor 1–6 (total 3–15). Motor: 6 obeys, 5 localises, 4 withdraws, 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none.$x$),
(84, $x$A GCS below what value is generally used as an indication for intubation?$x$, $x$<8$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: <8
A GCS of 8 or less is the general sign that the patient cannot protect their own airway. The tongue may fall back and block the airway, the gag and cough reflexes are weak, and the patient may aspirate vomit. Intubation protects the airway, prevents aspiration, and allows control of oxygen and CO₂ (important in head injury). Say "GCS less than 8, intubate".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <12 — Below 12 is moderate impairment; many patients can still protect their airway.
• <10 — Below 10 is too high a threshold for routine intubation.
• <5 — Below 5 is too low; waiting this long risks aspiration and hypoxic brain damage.

⭐ Key point: Also intubate for a rapidly falling GCS, poor oxygenation or ventilation, seizures that can't be controlled, or before transfer of a deteriorating head-injured patient.$x$),
(85, $x$The following statement regarding extradural haematoma are true EXCEPT$x$, $x$Prognosis is poor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prognosis is poor
The question asks which statement is NOT true. Extradural haematoma usually has a GOOD prognosis if it is found and drained quickly, because the brain beneath is often not badly damaged. The statement "prognosis is poor" is therefore false. Delay is what makes it fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is often caused by a skull fracture with laceration of the middle meningeal artery — It IS usually caused by a skull fracture tearing the middle meningeal artery (true).
• It commonly occurs at the pterion — It DOES commonly occur at the pterion, the thin part of the temporal skull (true).
• There is often a lucid interval — There IS often a lucid interval: knocked out, wakes up and talks, then quickly deteriorates (true).

⭐ Key point: Extradural = arterial bleed, lens-shaped (biconvex) on CT, lucid interval, emergency surgery. Subdural = venous bleed, crescent-shaped, often in elderly and alcoholics.$x$),
(86, $x$Cushing's triad, a late sign of rising intracranial pressure, consists of which three findings?$x$, $x$Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations
Cushing's triad is a LATE sign of raised ICP and approaching brainstem herniation. 1) HYPERTENSION with a WIDE pulse pressure: the body raises BP to push blood into the brain against high pressure. 2) BRADYCARDIA: a reflex response to the high BP and brainstem compression. 3) IRREGULAR breathing (Cheyne–Stokes, apnoea): compression of the breathing centre in the brainstem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnea — Low BP, fast pulse and fast breathing describe SHOCK, not raised ICP.
• Hypertension with tachycardia and hyperthermia — High BP with fast pulse and fever does not fit; the pulse is SLOW in Cushing's triad.
• Hypotension, bradycardia, and apnea — Low BP, slow pulse and apnoea is a pre-terminal picture, not Cushing's triad.

⭐ Key point: Cushing's triad means herniation is near. Call for help at once and prepare for urgent ICP-lowering treatment (osmotherapy, neurosurgery).$x$),
(87, $x$The Kellie-Monro (Monro-Kellie) Doctrine states which of the following?$x$, $x$The skull is a rigid, non-expansile compartment; an increase in volume of one intracranial component requires a reciprocal decrease in another to keep ICP constant$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The skull is a rigid, non-expansile compartment; an increase in volume of one intracranial component requires a reciprocal decrease in another to keep ICP constant
The Monro–Kellie doctrine says the skull is a rigid, closed box containing three things: brain (about 80%), blood (about 10%) and CSF (about 10%). The total volume must stay constant. If one part increases (a tumour, a bleed, swelling), another must decrease, for example CSF is pushed into the spinal canal or venous blood leaves. When these compensations run out, ICP rises steeply.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The brain can expand infinitely without pressure changes — The brain cannot expand freely inside the rigid skull.
• CSF volume is fixed and cannot change — CSF volume does change; displacing CSF is the first compensation.
• Cerebral blood flow is independent of ICP — Cerebral blood flow depends on CPP (MAP − ICP), so it is not independent of ICP.

⭐ Key point: The ICP volume–pressure curve is flat at first, then steep. Once compensation is used up, a small extra volume (coughing, suction) causes a big rise in ICP.$x$),
(88, $x$Signs of raised intracranial pressure include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Loss of hearing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Loss of hearing
The question asks for the sign that does NOT belong. Loss of hearing is not a sign of raised ICP. Raised ICP classically causes headache that is worse in the morning (CO₂ rises during sleep and on lying down), nausea and vomiting (often projectile), papilloedema (swollen optic discs), visual changes, a falling GCS, pupil changes and Cushing's triad late.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Early-morning headache — Early-morning headache IS a classic sign of raised ICP.
• Nausea and vomiting — Nausea and vomiting ARE classic signs of raised ICP.
• Papilloedema — Papilloedema IS a classic sign of raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: The EARLIEST and most sensitive sign of raised ICP is a change in the level of consciousness (restlessness, confusion, drowsiness).$x$),
(89, $x$The signs of an upper motor neuron lesion include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Fibrillations$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fibrillations
The question asks for the sign that does NOT belong. Fibrillations (tiny spontaneous twitches of single muscle fibres, seen on EMG) and fasciculations are signs of a LOWER motor neurone lesion, not an upper one. Upper motor neurone lesions (brain or spinal cord: stroke, MS, spinal injury) cause spasticity, increased (brisk) reflexes, clonus and a positive Babinski sign, with little muscle wasting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Muscular spasticity — Spasticity IS an upper motor neurone sign.
• Brisk deep tendon reflexes — Brisk deep tendon reflexes ARE an upper motor neurone sign.
• Positive Babinski response — A positive Babinski sign (big toe goes up) IS an upper motor neurone sign.

⭐ Key point: UMN = spastic, brisk reflexes, upgoing toe. LMN = floppy (flaccid), lost reflexes, wasting, fasciculations and fibrillations (as in GBS, polio and peripheral nerve injury).$x$),
(90, $x$A Licox brain tissue oxygen monitor measures which of the following?$x$, $x$Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2)
The Licox probe is placed in the brain tissue (white matter) and measures the partial pressure of oxygen there (PbtO₂), and also brain temperature. It shows whether the brain is getting enough oxygen. Normal PbtO₂ is about 20–35 mmHg; below 20 needs treatment and below 10–15 means serious brain hypoxia with a poor outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intracranial pressure only — ICP is measured by a separate probe or an EVD; Licox measures oxygen.
• Cerebral blood flow velocity — Cerebral blood flow velocity is measured by transcranial Doppler.
• Serum oxygen saturation — Serum (arterial) oxygen saturation is measured by an ABG or pulse oximeter.

⭐ Key point: To improve low PbtO₂: raise FiO₂, raise CPP (MAP), treat high ICP, correct anaemia, avoid hyperventilation (low CO₂), and treat fever and seizures.$x$),
(91, $x$Causes of Brain death are EXCEPT$x$, $x$Good brain oxygenation.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Good brain oxygenation.
The question asks which is NOT a cause of brain death. Good brain oxygenation keeps brain cells alive; it is the opposite of what causes brain death. Brain death happens when the whole brain, including the brainstem, is irreversibly destroyed, usually by severe injury, swelling or loss of blood and oxygen supply.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An acute catastrophic brain injury. — An acute catastrophic brain injury (trauma, a huge bleed) IS a cause.
• Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion — Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion (very high ICP stopping blood flow) IS a cause.
• Extracranial brain injury, as seen in patients following cardiopulmonary arrest — Brain injury from outside the skull, such as lack of oxygen after cardiac arrest (hypoxic-ischaemic injury), IS a cause.

⭐ Key point: Before testing for brain death, exclude reversible causes: sedative drugs, hypothermia, severe metabolic or endocrine problems, and neuromuscular blockers.$x$),
(92, $x$Brudzinski's sign is positive when which of the following occurs?$x$, $x$Involuntary flexion of the hips and knees when the examiner passively flexes the patient's neck$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Involuntary flexion of the hips and knees when the examiner passively flexes the patient's neck
Brudzinski's sign tests for meningeal irritation (meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). With the patient lying flat, the examiner gently bends the patient's head forward toward the chest. If the patient's hips and knees bend up on their own (involuntarily), the sign is positive. Bending the neck stretches the inflamed meninges, and the body bends the legs to reduce the stretch.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pain on hip flexion with knee extension — Pain on hip flexion with the knee straight describes a different test (Kernig's sign checks pain or resistance when straightening the knee with the hip bent).
• Dilation of the pupils with light exposure — Pupils dilating with light is abnormal but has nothing to do with Brudzinski's sign (pupils normally constrict).
• Rigid extension of the leg — A rigid straight leg is not the Brudzinski response.

⭐ Key point: Meningitis signs: neck stiffness, photophobia, fever, headache, Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs, and a purpuric rash in meningococcal disease. Give antibiotics without delay and use droplet precautions.$x$),
(93, $x$What is the recommended treatment for a patient with Guillain-Barré syndrome?$x$, $x$Plasmapheresis or immunoglobulin therapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Plasmapheresis or immunoglobulin therapy
Guillain–Barré syndrome is an autoimmune attack on peripheral nerves, often after an infection. Proven treatments that shorten the illness and improve recovery are PLASMA EXCHANGE (plasmapheresis, which removes the harmful antibodies) or IV IMMUNOGLOBULIN (IVIG, 0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days). Both work equally well; they are not given together. Supportive ICU care (breathing, autonomic monitoring, DVT prevention, pain control, nutrition) is essential.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics do not treat the immune nerve damage (the triggering infection has usually passed).
• Hospitalization for observation — Observation alone is not enough for significant weakness; specific treatment should start early.
• No treatment is required — GBS can paralyse the breathing muscles, so leaving it untreated is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Steroids do NOT help GBS. Monitor FVC every 4 hours: below 20 mL/kg needs ICU; below 15 mL/kg usually needs intubation.$x$),
(94, $x$What are common initial presenting symptoms of Myasthenia Gravis?$x$, $x$Ptosis and diplopia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ptosis and diplopia
Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies block the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. Muscles tire quickly with use and recover with rest. The EYE muscles are usually affected first: drooping eyelids (PTOSIS) and double vision (DIPLOPIA) are the commonest first symptoms (in about 2 out of 3 patients). Weakness is typically worse at the end of the day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Foot drop — Foot drop is more typical of a peripheral nerve problem (such as common peroneal nerve palsy).
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is not a feature of myasthenia (smooth muscle is spared).
• Severe headache — Severe headache is not a feature of myasthenia.

⭐ Key point: In a myasthenic crisis (breathing or swallowing failure), monitor FVC, protect the airway, and give IVIG or plasma exchange. Avoid aminoglycosides and magnesium.$x$),
(95, $x$A tetanus patient should ideally be nursed in an environment that is:$x$, $x$Quiet and dark, avoiding unnecessary stimulation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Quiet and dark, avoiding unnecessary stimulation
In tetanus, the toxin (tetanospasmin) blocks the nerves that normally relax muscles. Any stimulus — noise, light, touch, even a draught — can trigger painful, violent muscle spasms, including laryngospasm, which can stop breathing. The patient is therefore nursed in a QUIET, DARK (dim) room, with minimal handling and procedures grouped together, usually with sedation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bright and stimulating — A bright, stimulating environment would trigger spasms.
• Noisy, for constant communication — Noise triggers spasms and must be avoided.
• Cold, to reduce metabolic rate — Cold can trigger spasms and does not help; temperature should be kept comfortable.

⭐ Key point: Tetanus treatment: wound debridement, tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, benzodiazepines and magnesium for spasms, ventilation if needed, and the tetanus vaccine (the illness does not give immunity).$x$),
(96, $x$What is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for status epilepticus?$x$, $x$IV benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: IV benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam)
Status epilepticus (a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery) is a medical emergency. The FIRST-line drug is an IV BENZODIAZEPINE, for example lorazepam 4 mg IV, which can be repeated once after 5–10 minutes. Alternatives without IV access: buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam. Benzodiazepines boost the calming GABA system and stop most seizures quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral phenytoin — Oral phenytoin is too slow; phenytoin (or levetiracetam or valproate) is given IV as SECOND-line.
• IV mannitol — Mannitol lowers ICP but does not stop seizures.
• IV vancomycin — Vancomycin is an antibiotic; it might be used for meningitis but does not stop seizures.

⭐ Key point: The steps are: ABC, oxygen, check glucose (give dextrose if low), benzodiazepine, then a second-line IV drug, then anaesthesia (propofol or thiopentone) with intubation if seizures continue past 20–30 minutes.$x$),
(97, $x$Which sedative agent is preferred over benzodiazepines in mechanically ventilated ICU patients, in part due to its lower associated delirium risk?$x$, $x$Dexmedetomidine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dexmedetomidine
Dexmedetomidine is an alpha-2 agonist that gives light, rousable sedation: the patient is calm but can wake and follow commands. It does not depress breathing. Studies show less delirium and shorter ventilation than with benzodiazepines (MENDS, SEDCOM). Guidelines prefer propofol or dexmedetomidine over benzodiazepines for ventilated ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that increases delirium.
• Midazolam — Midazolam is a benzodiazepine that builds up (especially in kidney failure) and increases delirium.
• Diazepam — Diazepam is a long-acting benzodiazepine that also increases delirium.

⭐ Key point: Dexmedetomidine side effects are bradycardia and hypotension. Benzodiazepines are mainly used for seizures and alcohol withdrawal.$x$),
(98, $x$Non-pharmacological management of delirium focuses on risk factors such as:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Delirium usually has several causes at once, so non-drug prevention tackles all the risk factors together. That includes treating dehydration and constipation, protecting sleep (night-time quiet, dim lights, clustered care), reorienting the patient (clock, calendar, glasses, hearing aids, family), communicating clearly, early mobilisation, good pain control, adequate nutrition, and removing catheters and lines early. All the groups listed are correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dehydration, constipation and sleep deprivation — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.
• Reorientation, clear communication and immobility — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.
• Pain control, nutrition and early removal of catheters — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.

⭐ Key point: A useful memory aid for the causes of delirium is "PINCH ME": Pain, Infection, Nutrition, Constipation, Hydration, Medication, Environment. Screen every shift with CAM-ICU.$x$),
(99, $x$How is TENS distinguished from Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)?$x$, $x$Percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment (TEN greater than 30%, SJS less than 10%)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment (TEN greater than 30%, SJS less than 10%)
SJS and TEN are the same disease at different severities, so the difference is the PERCENTAGE of body surface area where the epidermis peels off. SJS is less than 10%, the SJS/TEN overlap is 10–30%, and TEN is more than 30%. Both are usually drug-induced, with fever, painful skin, mucosal ulcers (mouth, eyes, genitals) and a positive Nikolsky sign. TEN has a much higher death rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Causative organism — The causative agent is usually the same type (drugs such as carbamazepine, phenytoin, allopurinol, sulphonamides, nevirapine), so it doesn't separate them.
• Patient age only — Both can occur at any age.
• Presence of fever — Fever occurs in both, so it doesn't distinguish them.

⭐ Key point: Stop the causing drug immediately. Manage like a major burn: warm room, fluids, non-stick dressings, eye and mouth care, nutrition and pain relief, ideally in a burns unit or ICU.$x$),
(100, $x$How is Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis Syndrome (TENS) best classified?$x$, $x$A severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous drug reaction with widespread epidermal detachment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous drug reaction with widespread epidermal detachment
Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction that affects the skin AND mucous membranes. The top layer of skin (epidermis) dies and peels off in sheets over more than 30% of the body, leaving raw areas like a scald burn. The patient loses fluid, protein and heat, and is at high risk of infection and sepsis. The death rate is 25–35% or more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A viral exanthem — It is not a viral rash (exanthem), although infections can occasionally trigger it.
• A bacterial skin infection — It is not a bacterial skin infection; it is an immune reaction to a drug.
• A chronic autoimmune skin condition unrelated to medications — It is an acute reaction, usually caused by a medication, not a chronic autoimmune disease.

⭐ Key point: Use the SCORTEN score to predict death risk. Care includes barrier nursing, warming, fluids, eye care by an ophthalmologist, and stopping all non-essential drugs.$x$),
(101, $x$A patient is on a ventilator for respiratory failure. The nurse knows that a high pressure alarm on the ventilator may indicate:$x$, $x$A kinked endotracheal tube or the patient is coughing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A kinked endotracheal tube or the patient is coughing
The high-pressure alarm sounds when the pressure needed to deliver the breath rises above the set limit. Something is blocking or resisting airflow: a kinked or bitten ETT, secretions (needs suction), coughing, the patient fighting the ventilator, bronchospasm, a pneumothorax, or water in the tubing. Check the patient first, then the tube, then the circuit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A leak in the circuit — A leak in the circuit causes a LOW-pressure alarm (pressure can't build up).
• The patient is disconnected from the ventilator — Disconnection causes a LOW-pressure or low-volume alarm.
• The patient's lung has re-expanded — Re-expansion of the lung would improve compliance and lower pressure, not trigger a high-pressure alarm.

⭐ Key point: HIGH pressure = something blocking (kink, secretions, cough, biting, bronchospasm, pneumothorax). LOW pressure = something leaking (disconnection, cuff leak, circuit leak).$x$),
(102, $x$End-tidal CO2 (capnography) waveform loss or a sudden flat waveform in an intubated patient most urgently suggests:$x$, $x$Tube displacement/dislodgement (e.g., esophageal intubation or extubation) or complete obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tube displacement/dislodgement (e.g., esophageal intubation or extubation) or complete obstruction
Waveform capnography shows CO₂ in every breath. A normal square waveform proves the tube is in the trachea and the patient is being ventilated. A SUDDEN loss or flat line in an intubated patient most urgently means: the tube has come out or moved into the oesophagus (no CO₂ from the lungs), the tube is completely blocked, the circuit has disconnected, or cardiac arrest. Check the patient at once ("no trace = wrong place").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ventilator function — Normal ventilator function would show a regular square waveform, not a flat line.
• Improved lung compliance — Better lung compliance would still show a normal waveform.
• Adequate ventilation — Adequate ventilation shows CO₂ on every breath; a flat line means none is reaching the sensor.

⭐ Key point: Respond with DOPE (Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment): check the tube position, the patient's chest movement and the pulse, and hand-ventilate with 100% O₂.$x$),
(103, $x$When intubating a patient with suspected cervical spine injury, the recommended technique to protect the spine is:$x$, $x$Manual in-line stabilization with the anterior portion of the cervical collar removed during the procedure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Manual in-line stabilization with the anterior portion of the cervical collar removed during the procedure
In suspected cervical spine injury, the head and neck must not move during intubation. An assistant holds the head in line with the body, using both hands on each side (manual in-line stabilisation, MILS). The FRONT of the hard collar is opened or removed so the mouth can open wide enough for the laryngoscope. Video laryngoscopes help get a good view with less neck movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Full neck extension for optimal glottic view — Full neck extension could worsen a spinal injury and cause paralysis.
• Flexion of the neck — Flexing the neck could also move an unstable fracture.
• Leaving the patient fully upright — Sitting the patient upright does not protect the spine and makes intubation harder.

⭐ Key point: Treat every major trauma patient as having a C-spine injury until it is cleared. Use jaw thrust (not head tilt) to open the airway.$x$),
(104, $x$Signs of weaning intolerance during a trial include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Improved mentation and stable heart rate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improved mentation and stable heart rate
The question asks which is NOT a sign of failing a weaning trial. Improved mentation (more alert) with a stable heart rate is a GOOD sign that the patient is tolerating the trial. Signs of failure include fast breathing (RR above 35), accessory muscle use, see-saw (paradoxical) breathing, sweating, anxiety or agitation, a rise in HR or BP of more than 20%, falling SpO₂ (below 90%) and rising CO₂.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Use of accessory muscles and paradoxical (abdominal) breathing — Accessory muscle use and paradoxical breathing ARE signs of failure (respiratory muscle fatigue).
• Diaphoresis and anxiety — Sweating (diaphoresis) and anxiety ARE signs of failure.
• Significant increase in heart rate or blood pressure — A significant rise in HR or BP IS a sign of failure.

⭐ Key point: If a spontaneous breathing trial fails, put the patient back on comfortable support, look for the reason (fluid overload, secretions, weakness, low phosphate, sedation), and try again the next day.$x$),
(105, $x$If a ventilated patient acutely desaturates and the cause is not immediately identified, an appropriate first-line nursing action while troubleshooting is to:$x$, $x$Disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen while assessing for the cause$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen while assessing for the cause
When a ventilated patient suddenly desaturates and you don't know why, take the ventilator out of the problem. DISCONNECT the patient and hand-ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen. This checks whether the problem is the ventilator or the patient. Squeezing the bag also tells you about the airway: a hard bag suggests blockage or pneumothorax. Then work through DOPE and call for help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leave the patient on the ventilator and wait — Waiting while the patient's oxygen falls can lead to cardiac arrest.
• Immediately extubate — Extubating without a clear reason may remove a working airway and make things worse.
• Turn off all alarms to focus — Alarms are important information; turning them off is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Keep a bag-valve-mask connected to oxygen at EVERY ventilated patient's bedside, always checked and ready.$x$),
(106, $x$Diffusion hypoxia after discontinuing nitrous oxide anaesthesia is best prevented by:$x$, $x$Administering 100% oxygen for several minutes at the end of the case before returning to room air$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Administering 100% oxygen for several minutes at the end of the case before returning to room air
Nitrous oxide is very soluble in blood. When it is stopped at the end of anaesthesia, large amounts rush OUT of the blood into the alveoli and dilute the oxygen there. If the patient breathes room air at that moment, the alveolar oxygen can drop dangerously, causing hypoxia (diffusion hypoxia, the Fink effect). Giving 100% oxygen for 5–10 minutes after stopping nitrous oxide prevents this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapidly increasing volatile agent concentration — Increasing the volatile agent deepens anaesthesia and doesn't solve the oxygen dilution.
• Hyperventilating the patient with room air — Hyperventilating with room air would still give low oxygen.
• Administering naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids; it doesn't prevent diffusion hypoxia.

⭐ Key point: Nitrous oxide also expands gas-filled spaces, so avoid it in pneumothorax, bowel obstruction, middle-ear surgery and air embolism.$x$),
(107, $x$A patient develops rapidly rising temperature, severe muscle rigidity, tachycardia, and hypercarbia shortly after receiving succinylcholine. The priority treatment is:$x$, $x$Dantrolene, which reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dantrolene, which reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum
These signs (rapidly rising temperature, muscle rigidity including the jaw, fast heart rate, rising EtCO₂ and acidosis) after suxamethonium or a volatile gas are MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA. It is an inherited muscle disorder in which calcium floods out of the muscle stores, causing uncontrolled contraction and heat production. DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV (repeated as needed) is the specific treatment; it stops the calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids and has no effect here.
• Neostigmine — Neostigmine reverses non-depolarising muscle relaxants and does not treat MH.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and has no effect here.

⭐ Key point: MH management: stop the trigger, call for help, give 100% oxygen at high flow, give dantrolene, cool the patient, treat high potassium and acidosis, and monitor urine (rhabdomyolysis). Warn the family about genetic testing.$x$),
(108, $x$What is Mendelson's syndrome?$x$, $x$Chemical pneumonitis resulting from aspiration of acidic gastric contents into the lungs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chemical pneumonitis resulting from aspiration of acidic gastric contents into the lungs
Mendelson's syndrome is a CHEMICAL pneumonitis caused by inhaling acidic stomach contents into the lungs, classically during general anaesthesia in obstetrics (Curtis Mendelson, 1946). Stomach acid (especially with pH below 2.5 and volume over 25 mL) burns the lung lining, causing wheeze, breathlessness, hypoxia and ARDS within hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bacterial pneumonia acquired during mechanical ventilation — VAP is a bacterial infection after 48 hours of ventilation, not chemical aspiration.
• An allergic reaction to inhalational anaesthetic agents — An allergic reaction to anaesthetic gases is a different problem.
• Pulmonary embolism occurring after prolonged bed rest — PE after bed rest is a blood clot, not aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Prevent aspiration with fasting, antacids or H₂ blockers (ranitidine or sodium citrate in obstetrics), rapid sequence induction, and extubating only when the patient is awake.$x$),
(109, $x$Pulmonary oxygen toxicity is most likely to develop with which exposure pattern?$x$, $x$FiO2 greater than about 60% sustained for more than 24-48 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: FiO2 greater than about 60% sustained for more than 24-48 hours
Oxygen toxicity damages the lung lining when a high oxygen concentration (FiO₂ above about 60%) is breathed for a long time (more than 24–48 hours). Free radicals injure the alveoli, causing inflammation, fluid leak and eventually fibrosis, much like ARDS. Absorption collapse (atelectasis) can also happen, because nitrogen is washed out of the alveoli.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Brief exposure to 100% oxygen for less than 5 minutes during pre-oxygenation — A brief exposure to 100% oxygen (such as pre-oxygenation for intubation) is safe.
• FiO2 of 21% (room air) for any duration — Room air (21%) does not cause oxygen toxicity.
• Intermittent low-flow nasal cannula oxygen at 2 L/min — Low-flow 2 L/min nasal oxygen gives only about 28% and does not cause toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Give the LOWEST FiO₂ that achieves the target SpO₂ (usually 94–98%, or 88–92% in CO₂ retainers). Use PEEP to allow a lower FiO₂ in ventilated patients.$x$),
(110, $x$What is the currently recommended target temperature range for targeted temperature management after cardiac arrest?$x$, $x$32 to 36 degrees C$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 32 to 36 degrees C
After return of circulation following cardiac arrest, patients who remain unconscious are kept at a controlled temperature to protect the brain. Current guidance is a target between 32 and 36 °C for at least 24 hours, and above all to AVOID FEVER (above 37.7 °C) for at least 72 hours. Fever after cardiac arrest worsens brain damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40 to 42 degrees C — 40–42 °C is hyperthermia, which harms the brain.
• 20 to 25 degrees C — 20–25 °C is severe hypothermia and causes arrhythmias and cardiac arrest.
• 38 to 39 degrees C — 38–39 °C is fever, which is exactly what should be prevented.

⭐ Key point: During cooling, watch for shivering (treat with sedation or paralysis), bradycardia, low potassium (which rises on rewarming), high glucose, bleeding and infection. Rewarm slowly, at about 0.25–0.5 °C per hour.$x$),
(111, $x$Which parameter setting is primarily responsible for oxygenation rather than ventilation (CO2 removal)?$x$, $x$FiO2 and PEEP$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: FiO2 and PEEP
Oxygenation (getting O₂ into the blood) is controlled mainly by FiO₂ (the oxygen concentration) and PEEP (keeps the alveoli open for gas exchange and increases surface area). Ventilation (removing CO₂) is controlled by MINUTE ventilation: respiratory rate × tidal volume. So to raise a low PaO₂, increase FiO₂ or PEEP; to lower a high PaCO₂, increase the rate or tidal volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory rate — Respiratory rate mainly changes CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume mainly changes CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• I:E ratio — I:E ratio has some effect on oxygenation but is not the main control; it is adjusted for special cases.

⭐ Key point: O₂ problem → change FiO₂ or PEEP. CO₂ problem → change rate or tidal volume. Memorise this for ventilator questions.$x$),
(112, $x$What is the standard adult IV/IO dose of epinephrine during cardiac arrest, repeated every 3 to 5 minutes?$x$, $x$1 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1 mg
In adult cardiac arrest, the dose of adrenaline (epinephrine) is 1 mg IV or IO (10 mL of 1:10,000), repeated every 3–5 minutes (every other 2-minute CPR cycle). In non-shockable rhythms (asystole, PEA) give it as soon as possible. In VF/pulseless VT, give it after the 3rd shock. Flush with 20 mL of saline.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1 mg — 0.1 mg is too small for an adult; doses of about this size are used for children (10 mcg/kg).
• 10 mg — 10 mg is ten times too high and dangerous.
• 100 mcg given only once — 100 mcg given once is too small and is not repeated as recommended.

⭐ Key point: Adrenaline 1 mg every 3–5 minutes. Amiodarone 300 mg after the 3rd shock, then 150 mg after the 5th. Anaphylaxis is different: 0.5 mg IM of 1:1000.$x$),
(113, $x$Which is a qSOFA criterion for sepsis?$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
qSOFA (quick SOFA) is a bedside screen outside ICU for patients with suspected infection who are at risk of poor outcomes. It has three items, scoring 1 each: respiratory rate of 22/min or more, systolic BP of 100 mmHg or less, and altered mental state (GCS below 15). A score of 2 or more suggests a higher risk of death and needs urgent review. All three listed criteria are part of qSOFA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• RR >22/min — Fast breathing alone is only one of the three criteria.
• SBP <100 mmHg — Low systolic BP alone is only one of the three criteria.
• Altered mental status — Altered mental status alone is only one of the three criteria.

⭐ Key point: Remember qSOFA as "HAT": Hypotension (SBP ≤100), Altered mentation, Tachypnoea (RR ≥22). Do not wait for a high score to give antibiotics if sepsis is suspected.$x$),
(114, $x$What is the typical initial fluid resuscitation approach for a patient in hypovolemic shock?$x$, $x$A rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloid solution while closely monitoring the hemodynamic response$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloid solution while closely monitoring the hemodynamic response
Hypovolaemic shock means there is not enough fluid in the blood vessels. The first treatment is to replace volume quickly with an isotonic crystalloid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's), for example a 500 mL bolus over 15 minutes (or 20 mL/kg), then reassess HR, BP, capillary refill, urine output and mental state. Repeat if needed. If the cause is bleeding, give BLOOD early and stop the bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Withholding fluids until blood pressure normalizes spontaneously — Waiting for the BP to recover by itself lets organs suffer and the patient may die.
• Administering diuretics to reduce circulating volume — Diuretics REMOVE fluid and would make hypovolaemia worse.
• A slow maintenance-rate infusion of hypotonic fluid — A slow drip of hypotonic fluid (5% dextrose) does not stay in the vessels and is far too slow.

⭐ Key point: Use two large-bore cannulas (14–16 G). In trauma, limit crystalloid and give blood products early (permissive hypotension, except in head injury).$x$),
(115, $x$A patient with multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS) following sepsis requires nursing care primarily focused on:$x$, $x$Comprehensive, systematic monitoring and support of all affected organ systems$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Comprehensive, systematic monitoring and support of all affected organ systems
In MODS, two or more organ systems fail together (such as lungs, kidneys, liver, heart, blood clotting and brain), usually after sepsis or major trauma. Each failing organ affects the others, so care must be COMPREHENSIVE and SYSTEMATIC: assess and support every system ("head to toe" or "A to E") — ventilation, haemodynamics, kidney replacement, nutrition, glucose control, infection control, skin, and family support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single-organ focus only, ignoring other systems — Focusing on one organ misses the others failing alongside it.
• No specific monitoring beyond routine vital signs — Routine vital signs alone are not enough; MODS needs invasive monitoring and lab checks.
• Withholding all interventions — Withholding care is not appropriate unless a decision about end-of-life care has been made with the family.

⭐ Key point: Mortality rises with each failing organ. Use the SOFA score to track the organs, and treat the cause (source control of infection).$x$),
(116, $x$A patient with pelvic fracture is at risk for:$x$, $x$A and B$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and B
Pelvic fractures cause both. MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE: the pelvis has large veins and arteries, and the pelvic space can hold several litres of blood. Unstable fractures (open book) can cause rapid shock and death. FAT EMBOLISM: fat from the bone marrow of large fractured bones (pelvis, femur) can enter the bloodstream and travel to the lungs, brain and skin, usually 24–72 hours later.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fat embolism — Fat embolism alone is only part of the answer.
• Massive hemorrhage — Massive haemorrhage alone is only part of the answer.
• Neurogenic shock — Neurogenic shock comes from spinal cord injury, not pelvic fracture.

⭐ Key point: Fat embolism triad: breathing difficulty (hypoxia), confusion, and a petechial rash (armpits, chest, conjunctiva). Early fixation of fractures reduces the risk.$x$),
(117, $x$A patient with multiple trauma has a pelvic fracture suspected on examination. The nurse should avoid which action that could worsen bleeding?$x$, $x$Repeatedly 'springing' or manually testing pelvic stability, which can dislodge clots and worsen haemorrhage$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Repeatedly 'springing' or manually testing pelvic stability, which can dislodge clots and worsen haemorrhage
Repeatedly pressing or "springing" the pelvis to check if it is stable can move the broken bone ends, tear vessels and dislodge clots that are slowing the bleeding. This can turn a controlled bleed into a fatal one. If a pelvic fracture is suspected (from the mechanism or signs), apply a pelvic binder and get imaging; avoid repeated examinations.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Applying a pelvic binder as indicated — Applying a pelvic binder at the level of the greater trochanters is CORRECT; it reduces pelvic volume and bleeding.
• Monitoring vital signs closely — Monitoring vital signs is correct and essential.
• Preparing for possible blood transfusion — Preparing blood products is correct, because large blood loss is expected.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid log-rolling patients with a suspected pelvic fracture more than needed, and do not insert a urinary catheter if there is blood at the urethral meatus (urethral injury) until it is checked.$x$),
(118, $x$A patient s/p TURP has pink-tinged urine and bladder spasms. This is:$x$, $x$Normal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Normal
After transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP), PINK-tinged urine (light bleeding) and BLADDER SPASMS (from the catheter balloon and the raw area) are expected in the first days. Continuous bladder irrigation through a three-way catheter keeps clots from forming, and anticholinergic drugs (such as oxybutynin) ease the spasms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Indicative of infection — Infection would cause fever, cloudy smelly urine and rising inflammatory markers, not just pink urine.
• Sign of hemorrhage — Haemorrhage would show bright red urine with clots, falling BP and rising HR; pink urine is not haemorrhage.
• Urinary retention — Retention would cause a full painful bladder with little drainage; check the catheter is draining.

⭐ Key point: After TURP, report bright red urine or clots, a blocked catheter (no drainage with pain), and signs of TURP syndrome (confusion, low sodium, low BP).$x$),
(119, $x$A patient with facial burns, singed nasal hair, and hoarseness of voice after a house fire should be closely monitored for:$x$, $x$Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction
Facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in the mouth, a hoarse voice, cough with black sputum, and a history of a fire in an ENCLOSED space all suggest INHALATION INJURY. Heat and smoke cause swelling of the upper airway that can rapidly block it within hours, especially once IV fluids start. These patients need early assessment by an anaesthetist and often EARLY intubation before the airway closes. Also check for carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific concern beyond the visible burns — There IS a specific concern: the airway can be lost quickly.
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety does not explain singed nasal hair and hoarseness.
• Only skin infection risk — Infection is a later risk; the immediate danger is airway obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Give 100% oxygen (also treats CO poisoning; SpO₂ is falsely normal in CO poisoning). Watch for stridor, which is a late, dangerous sign.$x$),
(120, $x$To prevent pressure ulcers, a critically ill patient should be turned every:$x$, $x$2 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2 hours
Turning the patient every 2 hours relieves pressure on bony areas (sacrum, heels, hips, back of the head) before the skin and tissue are damaged by lack of blood flow. Pressure ulcers can start within 1–2 hours of unrelieved pressure in critically ill patients, who are immobile, poorly perfused and often on vasopressors. Turn the patient alternately (left 30°, back, right 30°), use pressure-relieving mattresses, and check the skin each time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 minutes — Every 30 minutes is impractical and would disturb rest and sleep.
• 1 hour — Every hour is more often than usually needed (though some very high-risk patients need it).
• 6 hours — Every 6 hours is far too long; ulcers would develop.

⭐ Key point: Use the Braden scale (a score of 18 or less means at risk), keep the skin clean and dry, ensure good nutrition, and remember devices (ETT, NG tube, masks) can also cause pressure ulcers.$x$),
(121, $x$The general adaptation syndrome of stress includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$A period of fast recovery$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A period of fast recovery
Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome has THREE stages: 1) ALARM (fight or flight: adrenaline and cortisol released, fast heart and breathing), 2) RESISTANCE (the body adapts and copes with the ongoing stressor), 3) EXHAUSTION (resources run out, leading to illness, burnout or even death if the stress continues). "A period of fast recovery" is not one of the stages.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An alarm reaction (fight-or-flight) — The alarm reaction IS the first stage.
• A resistance phase — The resistance phase IS the second stage.
• A period of exhaustion — The exhaustion phase IS the third stage.

⭐ Key point: Critical illness keeps patients in prolonged stress (high cortisol, high glucose, muscle breakdown). Staff are also at risk of exhaustion and burnout.$x$),
(122, $x$A critically ill patient in septic shock remains hypotensive despite adequate fluid resuscitation and high-dose vasopressors. Which endocrine abnormality should be considered and evaluated?$x$, $x$Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)
In severe sepsis, the adrenal glands may not produce enough cortisol for the body's needs, or the tissues may not respond to it. This is called critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency). Without enough cortisol, the blood vessels respond poorly to vasopressors and BP stays low. Low-dose hydrocortisone (200 mg/day) is recommended in septic shock needing ongoing vasopressors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary hyperaldosteronism — Primary hyperaldosteronism causes HIGH BP and low potassium, not refractory shock.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism causes fast heart rate and high output, but it is not the classic cause here.
• Growth hormone excess — Growth hormone excess (acromegaly) is a chronic condition, not a cause of shock.

⭐ Key point: Signs that point to adrenal insufficiency: low BP not responding to fluids and vasopressors, low sodium, high potassium, low glucose and a history of steroid use.$x$),
(123, $x$The following are indication of monitoring intra-abdominal pressure except:$x$, $x$Laparotomy closed with normal tension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Laparotomy closed with normal tension
The question asks which is NOT an indication for monitoring intra-abdominal pressure. Measuring IAP (via the bladder) is recommended for patients at risk of intra-abdominal hypertension: shock needing large-volume resuscitation, damage control laparotomy, major burns (above 25% TBSA), severe pancreatitis, massive transfusion, and abdominal surgery closed under tension. A laparotomy closed with NORMAL tension is low risk, so it is not an indication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shock requiring resuscitation. — Shock needing large fluid resuscitation IS an indication (the gut swells).
• Damage control laparotomy — Damage control laparotomy IS an indication (packs, swollen bowel).
• Major burns \> 25% — Major burns above 25% IS an indication (huge fluid volumes cause gut and wall oedema).

⭐ Key point: Normal IAP is 5–7 mmHg. IAH is 12 mmHg or more. Abdominal compartment syndrome is above 20 mmHg with new organ failure, which needs decompression.$x$),
(124, $x$Which of the following best describes the purpose of stress-ulcer prophylaxis (e.g. proton pump inhibitors) in critically ill patients?$x$, $x$To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients
Critically ill patients, especially those ventilated for more than 48 hours or with clotting problems, can develop stress ulcers in the stomach because blood flow to the stomach lining is reduced. These can bleed. Stress ulcer prophylaxis with a PPI (omeprazole, pantoprazole) or H₂ blocker reduces stomach acid and lowers the risk of significant upper GI bleeding. Early enteral feeding also helps protect the stomach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To treat active pneumonia — Stress ulcer drugs do not treat pneumonia; reducing stomach acid may even slightly increase pneumonia risk.
• To lower blood glucose — PPIs do not lower blood glucose.
• To prevent deep vein thrombosis — DVT is prevented with LMWH or heparin and mechanical devices, not PPIs.

⭐ Key point: Review stress ulcer prophylaxis daily and stop it when the patient no longer has risk factors; long-term use increases C. difficile and pneumonia risk.$x$),
(125, $x$Hemorrhagic discoloration of the flanks is known as$x$, $x$Grey-Turner's sign$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Grey-Turner's sign
Grey-Turner's sign is bruising (bluish discolouration) of the FLANKS. Blood from the retroperitoneum tracks along the tissue planes to the skin of the sides, typically 24–48 hours after the bleed. It is seen in severe (haemorrhagic) acute pancreatitis, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and retroperitoneal bleeding (including ruptured ectopic pregnancy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Grey-Prumer's sign — "Grey-Prumer's sign" does not exist; it is a made-up distractor.
• Cullen's sign — Cullen's sign is bruising around the UMBILICUS (navel), not the flanks.
• Kullen's sign — "Kullen's sign" is a misspelling distractor of Cullen's sign.

⭐ Key point: Grey-Turner = flanks; Cullen = umbilicus. Both suggest bleeding in the abdomen or retroperitoneum (pancreatitis, ruptured ectopic, AAA).$x$),
(126, $x$A hallmark early clinical clue suggesting necrotising fasciitis, distinguishing it from simple cellulitis, is:$x$, $x$Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings
Necrotising fasciitis is a fast-spreading bacterial infection of the deep tissues (fascia) that destroys tissue under the skin before the skin itself looks very bad. The early classic clue is severe PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to what you can see on the skin. Later signs include rapid spread, tense swelling, dusky or purple skin, blisters, crepitus (gas under the skin), numbness and sepsis. It is a surgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild, well-localised redness with minimal pain — Mild, well-localised redness with little pain fits simple cellulitis, not necrotising fasciitis.
• Slow onset over several weeks — Necrotising fasciitis spreads over HOURS, not weeks.
• Complete absence of systemic symptoms — Patients quickly become very unwell (fever, fast pulse, low BP, confusion); systemic symptoms are usually present.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is urgent surgical debridement (removing dead tissue) plus broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and sepsis care. Delay increases death and amputation.$x$),
(127, $x$The 'Golden Hour' concept in trauma care refers to:$x$, $x$The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival
The "golden hour" is the first 60 minutes after a major injury. During this time, rapid assessment (primary survey ABCDE), resuscitation, stopping bleeding and getting the patient to definitive care (surgery, CT, ICU) give the best chance of survival. Many trauma deaths from bleeding and airway problems are preventable if treated quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The first hour after hospital discharge — The time after hospital discharge has nothing to do with the golden hour.
• The hour before surgery begins — The hour before surgery is not the definition; the clock starts at the time of INJURY.
• A 60-minute waiting period before treatment can start — The golden hour is about acting FAST, not waiting.

⭐ Key point: Trauma deaths come in three peaks: immediate (unsurvivable), early (minutes to hours, the golden hour, preventable), and late (days to weeks, from sepsis and multi-organ failure).$x$),
(128, $x$A common complication of severe hypothermia is:$x$, $x$Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)
In severe hypothermia (core temperature below 28–30 °C), the heart becomes cold and very irritable. The heart rate slows, and the risk of arrhythmias rises sharply: atrial fibrillation, then VF, then asystole. Even moving the patient roughly can trigger VF. The ECG may show J (Osborn) waves. Cardiac arrest is the most serious complication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperactivity — Hypothermia causes slowing and drowsiness, not hyperactivity.
• Increased body temperature — Hypothermia means LOW body temperature, by definition.
• Rapid breathing — Breathing becomes slow and shallow in severe hypothermia, not rapid.

⭐ Key point: Handle hypothermic patients gently, rewarm (warm fluids, forced-air warming, ECMO in severe cases), and remember "no one is dead until warm and dead". Defibrillation and drugs work poorly below 30 °C.$x$),
(129, $x$A properly functioning underwater-seal drain typically shows:$x$, $x$Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration
In a working underwater-seal drain, the water level in the water-seal chamber rises and falls with breathing (swinging or tidaling), because pressure in the chest changes with each breath. This proves the tube is open and connected to the pleural space. INTERMITTENT bubbling, especially on coughing or breathing out, is expected while air is still leaving the chest after a pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber — CONTINUOUS bubbling in the water-seal chamber suggests an air leak (from the lung or the system) and must be checked.
• No movement at all in the water-seal chamber — No movement at all suggests the tube is blocked or kinked, or the lung has fully re-expanded.
• A water level that never moves — A water level that never moves means no swinging, so the drain may not be working.

⭐ Key point: Swinging = the drain is open. No swing = check for kinks, clots, or lung re-expansion. Continuous bubbling = look for an air leak. Never lift the bottle above the chest.$x$),
(130, $x$A key element of the "ventilator bundle" (VAP prevention bundle) includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes
The question asks which is NOT part of the VAP bundle. Routine, scheduled ventilator circuit changes do NOT reduce VAP. Breaking the circuit may actually increase contamination. Circuits are changed only when visibly soiled or faulty. The VAP bundle includes head-of-bed elevation, daily sedation holds with weaning assessment, peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis, and chlorhexidine oral care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Head-of-bed elevation 30-45 degrees — Head-of-bed elevation to 30–45° IS part of the bundle; it reduces aspiration.
• Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment — Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment IS part of the bundle; it shortens ventilation.
• Peptic ulcer disease and deep vein thrombosis prophylaxis — Peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis ARE part of the original IHI ventilator bundle.

⭐ Key point: Other VAP measures: subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, closed suction, hand hygiene, and early extubation.$x$),
(131, $x$All are signs of ruptured ectopic pregnancy EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Hyperglycaemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperglycaemia
The question asks which is NOT a sign of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Hyperglycaemia (high blood sugar) has no link with it. A ruptured ectopic causes internal bleeding into the abdomen: sudden lower abdominal pain (often one-sided), vaginal bleeding (usually light), shoulder-tip pain (blood irritating the diaphragm), dizziness, and signs of shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse — Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse ARE signs; they come from internal bleeding.
• Abdominal pain — Abdominal pain IS a sign, often the first symptom.
• Vaginal bleeding — Vaginal bleeding IS a sign, though often less than the internal bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Any woman of childbearing age with abdominal pain and shock has a ruptured ectopic until proven otherwise. Do a pregnancy test, resuscitate, cross-match, and arrange emergency surgery.$x$),
(132, $x$A pregnant patient at 32 weeks presents with painless, bright red vaginal bleeding. This presentation is most concerning for:$x$, $x$Placenta praevia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Placenta praevia
Placenta praevia means the placenta lies low in the uterus, over or near the cervix. As the lower uterus stretches in the third trimester, the placenta separates and bleeds. The classic sign is PAINLESS, BRIGHT RED vaginal bleeding, often recurrent, with a soft, non-tender uterus. The baby's head may not be engaged, or the lie may be abnormal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placental abruption — Placental abruption causes PAINFUL bleeding with a hard, tender ("woody") uterus.
• Normal spotting in pregnancy — Bleeding at 32 weeks is never "normal spotting"; it must be investigated.
• Round ligament pain — Round ligament pain causes sharp groin pain, not bleeding.

⭐ Key point: In placenta praevia NEVER do a vaginal examination (it can cause torrential bleeding). Confirm with ultrasound, resuscitate, and plan a caesarean section.$x$),
(133, $x$What are the symptoms of premature rupture of membranes?$x$, $x$All of the options$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
Premature rupture of membranes (PROM) is when the amniotic sac breaks before labour starts. Women may notice a sudden GUSH of fluid or a slow, constant leak or trickle that they can't control. Normal amniotic fluid is clear (or pale straw-coloured) and odourless. All the options describe PROM, so "all of the options" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feeling a gush of fluid from the vagina — A gush of fluid is one sign, but not the only one listed.
• Slow leak or trickle of fluid — A slow leak or trickle is one sign, but not the only one listed.
• The fluid is clear and odourless — Clear, odourless fluid is one feature, but not the only one listed.

⭐ Key point: Green fluid = meconium (fetal distress). Foul-smelling fluid with fever = chorioamnionitis (infection). After PROM, avoid digital vaginal exams, check for infection, and give antibiotics as per protocol.$x$),
(134, $x$A cervical incompetence diagnosis in a pregnant patient primarily increases the risk of:$x$, $x$Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation
Cervical incompetence (cervical insufficiency) means the cervix is weak and opens painlessly in the second trimester, without contractions. The pregnancy can be lost suddenly (second-trimester miscarriage) or the baby born very early. Treatment includes cervical cerclage (a stitch around the cervix) or vaginal progesterone, with monitoring of cervical length by ultrasound.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Post-term pregnancy — Cervical incompetence leads to EARLY birth, not post-term pregnancy.
• Gestational diabetes — Gestational diabetes is a metabolic condition, not related to the cervix.
• Multiple pregnancy — Multiple pregnancy is a cause of preterm birth, not a result of cervical incompetence.

⭐ Key point: History is the clue: repeated painless second-trimester losses. The cerclage stitch is removed at about 36–37 weeks or when labour starts.$x$),
(135, $x$A Rhesus-negative mother delivers a Rhesus-positive baby. The nurse anticipates administration of:$x$, $x$Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies
When an Rh-negative mother delivers an Rh-positive baby, some of the baby's red cells enter her blood. Her immune system can make anti-D antibodies (sensitisation). In a FUTURE pregnancy with an Rh-positive baby, these antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the baby's red cells (haemolytic disease of the newborn). Giving ANTI-D immunoglobulin to the MOTHER within 72 hours of delivery removes the baby's cells before her body reacts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific treatment is needed — Without treatment, the next Rh-positive baby is at serious risk.
• Anti-D immunoglobulin to the baby — Anti-D is given to the MOTHER, not the baby.
• Oxytocin to the mother for this purpose — Oxytocin contracts the uterus; it does not prevent Rh sensitisation.

⭐ Key point: Also give anti-D after any sensitising event (miscarriage, ectopic, amniocentesis, abdominal trauma, bleeding) and routinely at 28 weeks in many units.$x$),
(136, $x$The most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage, among the 4 Ts, is:$x$, $x$Tone (uterine atony)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tone (uterine atony)
After birth, the uterus must contract firmly to squeeze shut the blood vessels where the placenta was attached. If it stays soft and relaxed (uterine ATONY), blood pours out. Poor TONE causes about 70% of PPH. Risk factors include a big baby, twins, polyhydramnios, long or very fast labour, grand multiparity, infection and drugs that relax the uterus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trauma — Trauma (tears of the cervix, vagina or perineum, uterine rupture) causes about 20%.
• Tissue — Tissue (retained placenta or membranes) causes about 10%.
• Thrombin — Thrombin (clotting problems such as DIC) causes about 1%.

⭐ Key point: First steps for atonic PPH: call for help, rub the uterus (fundal massage), give oxytocin, empty the bladder, start IV fluids, and give tranexamic acid.$x$),
(137, $x$What important modification should be made when performing CPR on a pregnant patient in later pregnancy?$x$, $x$Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return
After about 20 weeks, the pregnant uterus presses on the inferior vena cava and aorta when the woman lies on her back (aortocaval compression). This sharply reduces blood returning to the heart, so CPR would be ineffective. Pushing the uterus to the LEFT by hand (manual left uterine displacement) while doing chest compressions relieves the pressure. A left lateral tilt of 15–30° is an alternative.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Place the patient in the prone position — The prone position (face down) makes CPR impossible.
• Avoid performing chest compressions entirely — Chest compressions are still essential in pregnancy.
• Compress the abdomen instead of the chest — Compressions are always on the CHEST (lower half of the sternum), never the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: In maternal cardiac arrest, if there is no return of circulation within 4 minutes, prepare for a perimortem caesarean section by 5 minutes. This helps both mother and baby.$x$),
(138, $x$Tracheo-oesophageal fistula is often associated with which other congenital anomaly?$x$, $x$Oesophageal atresia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oesophageal atresia
Tracheo-oesophageal fistula (TOF) is an abnormal connection between the trachea and the oesophagus. In about 85% of cases, it occurs together with oesophageal ATRESIA, where the upper oesophagus ends in a blind pouch. The baby has frothy saliva, chokes and coughs or turns blue on feeding, and an NG tube cannot be passed into the stomach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cleft lip only — Cleft lip can occur with other anomalies, but it is not the classic partner of TOF.
• Club foot only — Club foot is a limb anomaly, not typically linked with TOF.
• Polydactyly only — Polydactyly (extra fingers) is not typically linked with TOF.

⭐ Key point: TOF may be part of VACTERL (Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheo-oesophageal, Renal, Limb anomalies). Keep the baby nil by mouth, head up, with continuous suction of the upper pouch until surgery.$x$),
(139, $x$Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency results in which potential maternal complication of hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Wernicke encephalopathy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Wernicke encephalopathy
In hyperemesis gravidarum, prolonged vomiting and poor intake use up thiamine (vitamin B1) stores, especially if IV glucose is given without thiamine. Thiamine deficiency causes Wernicke's encephalopathy: confusion, ataxia (unsteady walking), and eye problems (nystagmus, ophthalmoplegia). It can lead to permanent memory loss (Korsakoff syndrome) or death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal failure — Renal failure can come from dehydration, but it is not caused by thiamine deficiency.
• Esophageal rupture — Oesophageal rupture (Boerhaave syndrome) can follow forceful vomiting, but not thiamine deficiency.
• Osmotic demyelination syndrome — Osmotic demyelination comes from correcting low sodium too fast, not thiamine deficiency.

⭐ Key point: Give THIAMINE before or with IV glucose in any malnourished patient (hyperemesis, alcohol misuse, starvation), and replace fluids and electrolytes.$x$),
(140, $x$A nurse is performing neonatal resuscitation. The newborn's heart rate is below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation. The next step is to:$x$, $x$Initiate chest compressions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Initiate chest compressions
Neonatal resuscitation focuses on ventilation, because most newborns need only effective breaths. If the heart rate is still BELOW 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of EFFECTIVE ventilation (chest is moving), START CHEST COMPRESSIONS. The ratio is 3 compressions to 1 breath (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute), using two thumbs on the lower third of the sternum, and increase oxygen to 100%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds — Continuing ventilation alone wastes time once the heart rate is below 60 with good ventilation.
• Administer epinephrine — Adrenaline is given only if the heart rate stays below 60 after about 30 seconds of good compressions and ventilation.
• Stop resuscitation and call the physician — Stopping resuscitation is wrong; call for help while continuing.

⭐ Key point: Newborn steps: dry, warm and stimulate; open the airway; give 5 inflation breaths; ventilate; compressions if HR is below 60; then drugs through an umbilical venous catheter.$x$),
(141, $x$The most widely used effective treatment for neonatal jaundice is:$x$, $x$Phototherapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Phototherapy
Phototherapy uses blue-green light (wavelength about 460–490 nm) to change bilirubin in the skin into a water-soluble form that the baby can pass in urine and stool without needing the liver. It is safe, non-invasive and effective for most babies with significant jaundice. Exchange transfusion is kept for very high or rapidly rising bilirubin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood transfusion — Blood transfusion does not lower bilirubin (exchange transfusion is a different, specialised procedure).
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics treat infection, which is only sometimes a cause of jaundice.
• Liver transplant — A liver transplant is only for rare liver diseases such as biliary atresia that fails surgery.

⭐ Key point: Phototherapy care: cover the eyes, expose as much skin as possible, turn the baby, give extra fluids or feeds, monitor temperature, and check bilirubin regularly. Jaundice within 24 hours of birth is always abnormal.$x$),
(142, $x$Pyloric stenosis in infants classically presents with:$x$, $x$Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age
Pyloric stenosis is thickening of the pylorus muscle (the stomach outlet), which blocks food from leaving the stomach. It usually starts at 3–6 weeks of age, often in first-born boys. Vomiting is PROJECTILE (forceful) and NON-BILIOUS (milky, not green), because the blockage is above where bile enters. The baby is hungry after vomiting, and an "olive" mass may be felt in the upper abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bilious vomiting from birth — Bilious (green) vomiting from birth suggests an obstruction BELOW the bile duct (such as duodenal atresia or malrotation).
• Chronic diarrhoea — Chronic diarrhoea is not a feature; babies are usually constipated.
• Bloody stools with fever — Bloody stools with fever suggest infection or intussusception.

⭐ Key point: Vomiting stomach acid causes a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. Correct fluids and electrolytes FIRST, then do surgery (pyloromyotomy).$x$),
(143, $x$Intussusception in infants classically presents with:$x$, $x$Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and 'redcurrant jelly' stools$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and 'redcurrant jelly' stools
Intussusception is when one part of the bowel telescopes into the next part, usually at the ileocaecal junction, in babies aged 3 months to 3 years. It causes episodes of severe colicky pain (the baby screams and draws up the legs, then is quiet between episodes), vomiting, and later "redcurrant jelly" stools (blood and mucus). A sausage-shaped mass may be felt in the abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless abdominal distension only — It is very painful, not painless distension.
• Chronic constipation with no acute symptoms — It is an acute emergency, not chronic constipation.
• A silent, asymptomatic abdominal mass — The baby is clearly unwell, so it is not a silent, asymptomatic mass.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, an NG tube, and air or contrast enema reduction. Surgery is needed if the enema fails or the bowel perforates.$x$),
(144, $x$Regarding the newborn which of this is correct?$x$, $x$The term newborn may be small for gestational age$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The term newborn may be small for gestational age
Small for gestational age (SGA) means a baby's weight is below the 10th centile FOR ITS GESTATIONAL AGE. A TERM baby (37–42 weeks) can still be SGA if it did not grow well in the womb (placental problems, maternal smoking, pre-eclampsia, infection). This statement is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preterms are babies delivered before 40 weeks of delivery — Preterm means born BEFORE 37 completed weeks, not before 40 weeks.
• All preterms are low birth weight — Not all preterm babies are low birth weight; a baby at 36 weeks can weigh more than 2500 g.
• All preterms are small for gestational age — Not all preterm babies are SGA; many preterm babies are an appropriate weight for their age.

⭐ Key point: Low birth weight is below 2500 g, very low is below 1500 g, extremely low is below 1000 g. SGA babies are at risk of low glucose, hypothermia and polycythaemia.$x$),
(145, $x$Which is part of pre-operative care for a child with Hirschsprung's disease?$x$, $x$Giving bowel washouts/enemas$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Giving bowel washouts/enemas
In Hirschsprung's disease, part of the bowel (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon) has no nerve cells, so it cannot relax and push stool along. Stool builds up above it, causing distension, constipation and risk of enterocolitis. Before surgery, regular bowel WASHOUTS (saline rectal irrigations) or enemas are given to empty the bowel, relieve distension and reduce infection risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CT scan — A CT scan is not routine before surgery; diagnosis is by rectal biopsy and contrast enema.
• Shaving the axillary hair — Shaving the axillary hair is not relevant to bowel surgery.
• Obtaining consent from the child himself — A child cannot legally give consent; parents or guardians consent.

⭐ Key point: Signs of Hirschsprung's: failure to pass meconium within 48 hours, distended abdomen, bilious vomiting. Watch for enterocolitis (fever, explosive foul diarrhoea), which is dangerous.$x$),
(146, $x$Fluid therapy in a severely dehydrated child typically begins with:$x$, $x$A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement
A severely dehydrated child (lethargic, weak pulse, cold hands, slow capillary refill, sunken eyes) is in or near shock. The first step is a RAPID IV (or IO) fluid bolus of isotonic crystalloid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's), commonly 10–20 mL/kg, repeated after reassessment. After the circulation is restored, the remaining deficit and maintenance fluids are replaced over 24–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral fluids only, regardless of severity — Oral fluids alone are for mild to moderate dehydration; severe dehydration needs IV fluids.
• Withholding all fluids until specialist review — Withholding fluid in severe dehydration can be fatal.
• Only subcutaneous fluids — Subcutaneous fluids are too slow for severe dehydration and shock.

⭐ Key point: The WHO plan for severe dehydration (Plan C) is 100 mL/kg of Ringer's lactate in phases. Watch for overload in malnourished children and those with heart disease.$x$),
(147, $x$A characteristic sign of croup is:$x$, $x$A barking cough with inspiratory stridor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A barking cough with inspiratory stridor
Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a viral infection (usually parainfluenza) of the larynx and trachea in children aged 6 months to 3 years. Swelling below the vocal cords narrows the airway, causing a BARKING ("seal-like") cough, hoarse voice and INSPIRATORY STRIDOR (a harsh sound when breathing in). It is often worse at night, with a mild fever.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A productive cough with green sputum — A productive cough with green sputum suggests a chest infection.
• Expiratory wheeze only — Expiratory wheeze suggests asthma or bronchiolitis (lower airway).
• High fever with drooling — High fever with drooling suggests EPIGLOTTITIS, which is more serious.

⭐ Key point: Treat croup with oral dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg); give nebulised adrenaline for severe stridor. Keep the child calm on the parent's lap.$x$),
(148, $x$Febrile convulsions in young children are most commonly associated with:$x$, $x$A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection
Febrile convulsions are seizures caused by fever in children aged 6 months to 5 years without brain infection. They are linked to a RAPID RISE in temperature, usually from a viral infection (such as an ear or throat infection, or roseola). Most are short (under 5 minutes) generalised seizures, and the child recovers fully.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic malnutrition alone — Chronic malnutrition alone does not cause febrile convulsions.
• Congenital heart disease exclusively — Congenital heart disease is not specifically linked to febrile convulsions.
• Vitamin overdose — Vitamin overdose is not a cause.

⭐ Key point: Care: keep the child safe, place in recovery position, time the seizure, give a benzodiazepine if it lasts over 5 minutes, and look for the cause of fever (exclude meningitis). Reassure parents that most children grow out of it.$x$),
(149, $x$A sickle cell disease patient presented with chest pain, low Spo2, cough, dyspnea, which of these is the cause?$x$, $x$Acute Chest Syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute Chest Syndrome
Acute chest syndrome is a serious complication of sickle cell disease. Sickled cells block the small lung vessels (plus infection or fat embolism), causing chest pain, cough, breathlessness, low SpO₂, fever, and a NEW shadow on the chest X-ray. It is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease and needs urgent treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute Asthmatic — Acute asthma causes wheeze and breathlessness, but this picture in sickle cell disease points to acute chest syndrome.
• Vaso-Occlusive Crisis — A vaso-occlusive crisis causes pain (often bones and joints) but not hypoxia with lung signs.
• Acute Anaemia — Acute anaemia causes tiredness, pallor and breathlessness, but not chest pain with a new lung shadow.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: oxygen, pain relief, IV fluids (careful), antibiotics, incentive spirometry, and blood or exchange transfusion if severe. Monitor SpO₂ closely.$x$),
(150, $x$Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) risk is reduced by which key recommendation?$x$, $x$Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)
Placing babies to sleep on their BACK (supine) is the most important way to reduce SIDS ("Back to Sleep"). Other safe sleep advice: a firm flat mattress, no pillows, toys or loose bedding, the baby's feet at the foot of the cot, room-sharing (not bed-sharing) for the first 6 months, a smoke-free home, breastfeeding, and no overheating.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placing infants to sleep prone (on their stomach) — Sleeping prone (on the stomach) INCREASES the risk of SIDS.
• Co-sleeping in the same bed as an adult at all times — Bed-sharing with adults increases the risk, especially if the adult smokes, drinks or takes sedatives.
• Using soft, loose bedding in the crib — Soft, loose bedding increases the risk of suffocation and SIDS.

⭐ Key point: SIDS peaks at 2–4 months of age. Teach all parents safe sleep before discharge.$x$),
(151, $x$Florence Nightingale's Environmental Theory, published in 'Notes on Nursing,' primarily focuses on which idea?$x$, $x$Nursing should focus on manipulating the client's environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nursing should focus on manipulating the client's environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur
Florence Nightingale's Environmental Theory says the nurse's main job is to manage the patient's ENVIRONMENT — fresh air, light, warmth, cleanliness, quiet, clean water, drainage and good food — so that nature can heal the body. She believed disease was helped or hindered by surroundings. Her ideas began modern infection control and patient comfort.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nursing should focus exclusively on administering medications prescribed by physicians — Giving medications is part of nursing, but Nightingale's theory is about the environment, not drug administration.
• Illness is caused solely by genetic predisposition and cannot be influenced by environment — Nightingale believed the environment strongly affects health, so illness is not "solely genetic".
• The nurse's primary role is to perform invasive procedures independent of physicians — Performing invasive procedures independently is not her theory.

⭐ Key point: Nightingale is called the founder of modern nursing; she wrote "Notes on Nursing" (1859) and improved care in the Crimean War through hygiene.$x$),
(152, $x$Which of the following is one of Henderson's 14 basic needs related to physiological function?$x$, $x$Breathe normally$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Breathe normally
Virginia Henderson described 14 basic needs that the nurse helps patients meet when they cannot meet them alone. The FIRST is "Breathe normally". Others include: eat and drink adequately, eliminate, move and keep a good posture, sleep and rest, dress, keep body temperature normal, keep clean, avoid danger, communicate, worship, work, play and learn.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Achieve self-actualization — Self-actualisation is the top of Maslow's hierarchy, not one of Henderson's needs.
• Maintain the flexible line of defense — The flexible line of defence comes from Neuman's Systems Model.
• Engage in role-function adaptation — Role-function adaptation comes from Roy's Adaptation Model.

⭐ Key point: Henderson's definition of nursing: helping the person "perform those activities contributing to health or its recovery that he would perform unaided if he had the necessary strength, will or knowledge".$x$),
(153, $x$Orem identified three types of nursing systems. One of them is:$x$, $x$Wholly compensatory$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Wholly compensatory
Dorothea Orem's self-care deficit theory describes three nursing systems depending on how much the patient can do for themselves. 1) WHOLLY COMPENSATORY: the nurse does everything (an unconscious, ventilated ICU patient). 2) Partly compensatory: the nurse and patient share care. 3) Supportive-educative: the patient can do the care with teaching and support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Team nursing — Team nursing is a way of organising staff, not one of Orem's systems.
• Primary nursing — Primary nursing is a care delivery model, not one of Orem's systems.
• Individualised client care — Individualised client care is a general concept, not one of Orem's three systems.

⭐ Key point: Most ICU patients start in the wholly compensatory system and move toward supportive-educative as they recover.$x$),
(154, $x$In Sister Callista Roy's Adaptation Model, the person is viewed as:$x$, $x$A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses
In Callista Roy's Adaptation Model, the person is an ADAPTIVE SYSTEM. Stimuli (focal, contextual and residual) are the input. The person's coping processes (the regulator, which is physiological, and the cognator, which is mental and emotional) process these stimuli. The output is behaviour, which is either ADAPTIVE (healthy) or INEFFECTIVE. Adaptation happens in four modes: physiological, self-concept, role function, and interdependence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A passive recipient of environmental changes with no internal processing mechanisms — Roy sees the person as active, not a passive recipient.
• A system defined solely by 14 basic needs — The 14 basic needs belong to Henderson, not Roy.
• An energy field indistinguishable from the environment — An energy field indistinguishable from the environment is Martha Rogers' view.

⭐ Key point: The goal of nursing in Roy's model is to promote adaptation in the four modes.$x$),
(155, $x$The following Stressors that disrupt the system according Neumann's model include ------ ------$x$, $x$Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors.
In Betty Neuman's Systems Model, stressors are classified by where they come from. INTRApersonal stressors arise within the person (such as pain or fear). INTERpersonal stressors arise between people (such as conflict with family or staff). EXTRApersonal stressors arise from outside the person (such as finances or the hospital environment). Stressors can break through the lines of defence and cause illness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The primary, secondary, and tertiary stressor. — Primary, secondary and tertiary are levels of PREVENTION (interventions) in Neuman's model, not types of stressors.
• The individuals, families, and groups stressor — Individuals, families and groups are possible CLIENTS in her model, not stressors.
• Physiological, sociocultural, and developmental stressor — Physiological, sociocultural and developmental are VARIABLES of the client system, not stressor categories.

⭐ Key point: Neuman's model includes the flexible line of defence (outer), the normal line of defence, lines of resistance, and the basic core structure.$x$),
(156, $x$A nurse using Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings as a guiding framework would most likely incorporate which of the following into practice?$x$, $x$Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields
Martha Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings sees each person as an energy field that is constantly exchanging energy with the environmental energy field. The nurse aims to promote harmony between the two fields. Practices like THERAPEUTIC TOUCH, relaxation, music and imagery come from this view.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strict enforcement of the 14 basic needs checklist — The 14 basic needs checklist belongs to Henderson.
• Ranking patient needs using Maslow's pyramid exclusively — Maslow's pyramid is a psychology theory, not Rogers' framework.
• Assessment limited only to the 11 functional health patterns — The 11 functional health patterns belong to Marjory Gordon.

⭐ Key point: Key Rogers concepts are energy fields, openness, pattern and pandimensionality (the principles of homeodynamics).$x$),
(157, $x$Marjory Gordon's Functional Health Patterns framework is primarily used by nurses to:$x$, $x$Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time
Marjory Gordon's 11 Functional Health Patterns give nurses a structured way to do a HOLISTIC nursing assessment. They cover health perception and management, nutrition and metabolism, elimination, activity and exercise, sleep and rest, cognition and perception, self-perception, role and relationships, sexuality and reproduction, coping and stress, and values and beliefs. The data then guide nursing diagnoses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnose medical conditions independently of physicians — Nurses do not diagnose medical conditions with this framework; they make NURSING diagnoses.
• Replace the nursing process entirely with a single-step evaluation — It supports the nursing process; it does not replace it.
• Calculate a client's position on the health-illness continuum numerically — It does not calculate a numerical position on a health–illness scale.

⭐ Key point: Gordon's patterns are widely used to organise admission assessments and link well with NANDA nursing diagnoses.$x$),
(158, $x$According to Maslow's hierarchy, the most basic needs are:$x$, $x$Physiological needs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Physiological needs
Maslow's hierarchy has five levels, from the bottom up: PHYSIOLOGICAL needs (air, water, food, sleep, elimination), then safety and security, love and belonging, esteem, and self-actualisation at the top. The physiological needs are the most basic and must be met first. In nursing, this is why AIRWAY, BREATHING and CIRCULATION come first when prioritising care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Safety and security needs — Safety and security are the second level.
• Self-actualisation needs — Self-actualisation is the TOP (highest) level.
• Social needs — Social (love and belonging) needs are the third level.

⭐ Key point: In exam questions about priority, choose the answer that meets a physiological need (especially oxygen and airway) before psychological or educational needs.$x$),
(159, $x$The standard format for writing a 'problem-focused' (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is often referred to as the PES format, meaning:$x$, $x$Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)
A problem-focused (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is written in PES format. P is the PROBLEM (the NANDA label, such as "Ineffective airway clearance"). E is the ETIOLOGY ("related to" thick secretions and weak cough). S is the SIGNS and SYMPTOMS, the defining characteristics ("as evidenced by" coarse crackles and SpO₂ 88%).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Plan, Evaluate, Solve — Plan, Evaluate, Solve is not the PES format.
• Patient, Environment, Standard — Patient, Environment, Standard is not the PES format.
• Priority, Evidence, Solution — Priority, Evidence, Solution is not the PES format.

⭐ Key point: A RISK nursing diagnosis has no "S" part (the problem hasn't happened yet), for example "Risk for infection related to central venous catheter".$x$),
(160, $x$Standardised nursing terminologies such as NANDA, NIC, and NOC exist to:$x$, $x$Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes
Standardised nursing languages give nurses a common, consistent vocabulary. NANDA-I covers nursing DIAGNOSES, NIC (Nursing Interventions Classification) covers nursing INTERVENTIONS, and NOC (Nursing Outcomes Classification) covers patient OUTCOMES. Using them improves communication, documentation, electronic records, research and showing the value of nursing care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Make documentation more variable between institutions — They make documentation MORE consistent, not more variable.
• Replace physician orders — They describe nursing care; they don't replace physician orders.
• Track hospital finances — They are not tools for tracking hospital finances.

⭐ Key point: Remember the trio: NANDA = diagnosis, NIC = intervention, NOC = outcome.$x$),
(161, $x$Which of the following goal statements is correctly written in SMART format?$x$, $x$"Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift"$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: "Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift"
A SMART goal is Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic and Time-bound. "Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 metres) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift" says who (the client), what (walk), how much (20 metres), under what condition (without shortness of breath) and by when (end of 3 days). It is patient-centred and measurable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• "Client will feel better soon" — "Feel better soon" is vague and not measurable or time-bound.
• "Nurse will help the client walk" — "Nurse will help the client walk" is a nursing action (intervention), not a patient goal.
• "Client's pain will improve" — "Pain will improve" has no measure (such as a pain score) and no time frame.

⭐ Key point: Write goals about the PATIENT, with an action verb, a measurable criterion and a time frame. Another useful format is RUMBA.$x$),
(162, $x$A retrospective nursing audit involves reviewing records:$x$, $x$After the patient has been discharged$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: After the patient has been discharged
A RETROSPECTIVE audit looks BACK at care after it has finished, by reviewing the patient's records after discharge. It checks whether care met the set standards (for example, whether every patient had a VTE assessment). It is easy to do but can't change care for that patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• During the care of the patient — Reviewing during the patient's care is a CONCURRENT audit.
• Before the patient is admitted to the hospital — Before admission there is no care to audit.
• To plan the patient's care before it begins — Planning care before it begins is care planning, not an audit.

⭐ Key point: Concurrent audit = during care (can correct problems as they happen). Retrospective audit = after discharge (looks for patterns to improve future care).$x$),
(163, $x$A 'null hypothesis' (H0) states that$x$, $x$There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied
The null hypothesis (H₀) states that there is NO relationship or difference between the variables being studied. For example: "There is no difference in VAP rates between patients who receive chlorhexidine mouth care and those who don't." Statistical tests check whether the data are strong enough to REJECT the null hypothesis (usually p below 0.05).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is definitely a significant relationship between the variables — Stating there IS a significant relationship describes the alternative (research) hypothesis.
• The researcher's prediction is automatically correct — No hypothesis is "automatically correct"; it must be tested with data.
• Data collection is unnecessary — Data are always needed to test a hypothesis.

⭐ Key point: H₀ = no difference. H₁ (alternative) = there is a difference. Rejecting H₀ wrongly is a Type I error; failing to reject a false H₀ is a Type II error.$x$),
(164, $x$Which variable is NOT manipulated by the researcher but measured as the outcome?$x$, $x$Dependent variable$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dependent variable
The DEPENDENT variable is the outcome that is MEASURED. Its value "depends" on the independent variable. The researcher doesn't change it directly. Example: in a study of whether early mobilisation shortens ICU stay, the length of stay is the dependent variable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extraneous variable — An extraneous variable is an outside factor that might affect the result and needs controlling.
• Independent variable — The independent variable is the one the researcher manipulates or changes (the cause), such as early mobilisation.
• Internal variable — "Internal variable" is not a standard research term.

⭐ Key point: Independent = cause (what you change). Dependent = effect (what you measure).$x$),
(165, $x$Probability sampling gives every member of the population:$x$, $x$An equal chance of being selected$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An equal chance of being selected
In PROBABILITY (random) sampling, every member of the population has a known, usually EQUAL, chance of being chosen, as in drawing names from a hat or using random number tables. This reduces selection bias and allows the results to be generalised to the whole population. Types include simple random, stratified random, systematic and cluster sampling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal representation — Equal representation isn't guaranteed; random sampling gives an equal CHANCE, but the sample may not match the population perfectly.
• Guaranteed selection — No one is guaranteed to be chosen in random sampling.
• No chance of selection — With no chance of selection, there would be no sampling at all.

⭐ Key point: Non-probability sampling (convenience, purposive, snowball, quota) is common in qualitative research but cannot be generalised as confidently.$x$),
(166, $x$A patient's body weight measured in kilograms is an example of which scale of measurement?$x$, $x$Ratio$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
Weight in kilograms is RATIO data. It has equal intervals between values and a TRUE ZERO (0 kg means no weight), so you can say "80 kg is twice as heavy as 40 kg". Other ratio examples: height, age, urine output, heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are names or categories without order (blood group, gender).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have an order but unequal gaps (pain scored mild, moderate, severe; GCS).
• Interval — Interval data have equal gaps but NO true zero (temperature in °C, where 0 °C is not "no temperature").

⭐ Key point: Remember NOIR: Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio, from least to most information.$x$),
(167, $x$The following are Sources of literature review EXCEPT$x$, $x$Social medias$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Social medias
The question asks which is NOT a proper source for a literature review. Social media (Facebook, WhatsApp, Instagram, TikTok) is not a reliable academic source: posts are not peer-reviewed and often contain opinions or misinformation. Good sources include journal articles, dissertations and theses, books, existing theories, reports and reputable internet databases (PubMed, CINAHL, Cochrane).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• dissertations — Dissertations ARE a valid source (they are reviewed academic work).
• existing theory — Existing theory IS a valid source and often frames the study.
• Internet — The internet can be a valid source when using reputable databases and websites (journals, WHO, government sites).

⭐ Key point: Prefer primary sources (original research reports) and recent, peer-reviewed literature (usually within the last 5–10 years).$x$),
(168, $x$The "mean" of a data set is:$x$, $x$The sum of all values divided by the number of values$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The sum of all values divided by the number of values
The MEAN is the arithmetic average: add up all the values and divide by how many values there are. Example: heart rates 70, 80 and 90 give (70 + 80 + 90) ÷ 3 = 80. The mean uses every value but is pulled by extreme values (outliers).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The most frequently occurring value — The most frequently occurring value is the MODE.
• The middle value when data are arranged in order — The middle value when the data are in order is the MEDIAN.
• The difference between the highest and lowest values — The difference between the highest and lowest values is the RANGE.

⭐ Key point: For skewed data (such as length of hospital stay), the median describes the middle better than the mean.$x$),
(169, $x$In most nursing and health research, a result is typically considered "statistically significant" when the p-value is:$x$, $x$Less than or equal to 0.05$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Less than or equal to 0.05
A p-value of 0.05 or less (p ≤ 0.05) is the usual cut-off for statistical significance in health research. It means there is a 5% or smaller probability that the result happened by chance if the null hypothesis were true. So the researcher rejects the null hypothesis and accepts that there is likely a real difference or relationship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Greater than 0.05 — A p-value above 0.05 is NOT significant; the result could easily be chance.
• Equal to 1.0 — p = 1.0 means no evidence at all against the null hypothesis.
• Greater than 0.5 — p above 0.5 is far from significant.

⭐ Key point: Statistical significance doesn't always mean clinical importance. Also look at the size of the effect and the confidence interval.$x$),
(170, $x$The Chi-square test is most appropriately used to analyse:$x$, $x$The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables
The chi-square (χ²) test analyses the relationship between CATEGORICAL (nominal or ordinal) variables, using counts or frequencies in a table. Example: is there an association between gender (male/female) and developing VAP (yes/no)? It compares the observed numbers with the numbers expected if there were no relationship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The correlation between two continuous variables — The correlation between two continuous variables uses Pearson's r (or Spearman's).
• The mean difference between two groups — The mean difference between two groups uses a t-test (or ANOVA for three or more groups).
• The standard deviation of a single sample — The standard deviation describes the spread of one sample; it is not a chi-square test.

⭐ Key point: Choose the test by data type: categories → chi-square; means of 2 groups → t-test; means of 3+ groups → ANOVA; relationship between 2 continuous variables → correlation.$x$),
(171, $x$A quasi-experimental design differs from a true experimental design mainly because it$x$, $x$Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable
A true experiment has three features: manipulation of the independent variable, a control group, and RANDOM assignment of participants to groups. A quasi-experimental design still tests an intervention but LACKS random assignment (and sometimes a control group), often because randomising is not possible or ethical in real wards. This makes it weaker for proving cause and effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never involves any form of intervention — Quasi-experiments DO involve an intervention.
• Always has a larger sample size than true experiments — Sample size is not what defines the design.
• Cannot be used to study cause-and-effect relationships at all — They can suggest cause and effect, though less strongly than RCTs.

⭐ Key point: The randomised controlled trial (RCT) is the strongest single study design for testing treatments. A systematic review of RCTs sits at the top of the evidence hierarchy.$x$),
(172, $x$A pilot study provides the opportunity to:$x$, $x$Test the research instrument$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Test the research instrument
A pilot study is a small trial run done before the main study. Its main purpose is to TEST the research instrument (questionnaire, checklist, equipment) and procedures: are the questions clear, how long does it take, does data collection work, is the tool reliable? Problems are fixed before the full study, saving time and money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Publish the results early — Pilot results are not meant for early publication; they test feasibility.
• Test the research subjects — Pilot studies test the methods, not the research subjects themselves.
• Understand the research problem — The research problem should be understood before the pilot, from the literature review.

⭐ Key point: Participants in the pilot are usually NOT included in the main study sample.$x$),
(173, $x$'Reliability' of a research instrument refers to$x$, $x$The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration
RELIABILITY is how CONSISTENT and STABLE an instrument is: it gives the same result when used again under the same conditions, or by different people. Example: a reliable pain scale gives similar scores if the patient's pain hasn't changed. Types include test–retest, inter-rater reliability and internal consistency (Cronbach's alpha, aim for 0.7 or above).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The degree to which the instrument measures the intended concept accurately — Measuring the intended concept accurately is VALIDITY, not reliability.
• The cost of producing and distributing the instrument — The cost of an instrument is unrelated to reliability.
• The number of items contained in a questionnaire — The number of items does not define reliability.

⭐ Key point: Reliability = consistency. Validity = accuracy (measures what it should). A tool can be reliable but not valid, but it cannot be valid without being reliable.$x$),
(174, $x$The first and most important function of a manager is:$x$, $x$Planning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Planning
PLANNING is the first and most basic function of management. It means deciding in advance what to do, how, when and by whom: setting goals and objectives, and choosing the best methods. All the other functions (organising, staffing, directing, controlling) depend on the plan. Without planning, work is reactive and disorganised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delegation — Delegation is a part of directing and organising; it follows planning.
• Funding — Funding (budgeting) is part of planning, not the first function itself.
• Supervision — Supervision is part of directing and controlling, which come after planning.

⭐ Key point: Fayol's functions of management, in order: Planning, Organising, Commanding (Directing), Coordinating, Controlling.$x$),
(175, $x$Henri Fayol's 14 principles include the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Strategic management$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Strategic management
The question asks which is NOT one of Fayol's 14 principles. "Strategic management" is a modern management concept, not one of Henri Fayol's 14 principles (1916). His principles include division of labour, authority and responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interest, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Division of labour — Division of labour IS one of his principles (specialisation improves efficiency).
• Discipline — Discipline IS one of his principles (respect for rules and agreements).
• Unity of command — Unity of command IS one of his principles (each worker has only one boss).

⭐ Key point: Fayol is the father of administrative (modern) management; Taylor is the father of scientific management.$x$),
(176, $x$The ‘father’ of Scientific Management is:$x$, $x$Frederick W. Taylor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Frederick W. Taylor
Frederick Winslow Taylor is the "father of scientific management" (Principles of Scientific Management, 1911). He used time-and-motion studies to find the "one best way" to do each task, selected and trained workers scientifically, and linked pay to output (piece-rate). His ideas aimed to increase efficiency and productivity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Henry Gantt — Henry Gantt was Taylor's associate and created the Gantt chart for scheduling.
• W. Edwards Deming — W. Edwards Deming is linked with Total Quality Management and the PDSA cycle.
• Henry Ford — Henry Ford introduced the moving assembly line in car manufacturing.

⭐ Key point: Classical management thinkers: Taylor (scientific management), Fayol (administrative management), Weber (bureaucracy).$x$),
(177, $x$In Herzberg's theory, the following are motivators EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Money (salary)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Money (salary)
Herzberg's two-factor theory separates MOTIVATORS (which create satisfaction and drive people to work harder: achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself, advancement and growth) from HYGIENE factors (which prevent dissatisfaction but don't motivate: salary/money, working conditions, company policy, supervision, job security, relationships). Money is a HYGIENE factor, so it is the exception.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Recognition — Recognition IS a motivator.
• Achievement — Achievement IS a motivator.
• Challenging work — Challenging work (the work itself) IS a motivator.

⭐ Key point: Poor pay causes dissatisfaction, but good pay alone does not motivate long-term. Recognition and growth do.$x$),
(178, $x$Which leadership style is most appropriate during a mass casualty emergency?$x$, $x$Autocratic$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Autocratic
In a mass casualty emergency there is no time for discussion. Quick, clear decisions and firm direction are needed to save lives. The AUTOCRATIC (authoritarian) style, where the leader makes decisions and gives direct orders, is most effective in emergencies, cardiac arrests and situations with inexperienced staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Democratic — The democratic style involves the team in decisions; it is good for planning but too slow in a crisis.
• Laissez-faire — The laissez-faire style gives little direction; it would be dangerous in an emergency.
• Affiliative — The affiliative style focuses on harmony and feelings, not quick decisions.

⭐ Key point: No leadership style is best for everything. Choose based on the situation (situational leadership). Transformational leadership is best for long-term improvement.$x$),
(179, $x$A nurse delegates the administration of intravenous medication to a nursing assistant. This action is:$x$, $x$Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant's scope of practice$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant's scope of practice
Giving IV medication requires knowledge, judgement and skills (drug calculations, checking compatibility, monitoring for reactions) that are within the scope of a registered nurse, not a nursing assistant. Delegating it is INAPPROPRIATE and unsafe. If harm occurs, the nurse who delegated is accountable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate — It is not appropriate, because the task is outside the assistant's role.
• Acceptable if supervised — Supervision cannot make a task acceptable if it is outside the person's legal scope.
• Recommended during emergencies — Emergencies do not allow staff to work outside their scope of practice.

⭐ Key point: The 5 rights of delegation: right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction or communication, right supervision. You can delegate tasks, but not accountability.$x$),
(180, $x$Which of this budget is not part types of budget?$x$, $x$Supplementary budget$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Supplementary budget
The main types of budgets in health care management are the OPERATIONAL (operating) budget for day-to-day running costs, the CAPITAL budget for large, long-lasting items like equipment and buildings, and the PERSONNEL budget for staff salaries and benefits (often part of the operating budget). A "supplementary budget" is a government term for extra funds added during the year, not a standard type of nursing unit budget.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personnel budget — The personnel budget IS a type (salaries, overtime, benefits).
• Operational budget — The operational budget IS a type (supplies, utilities, daily running).
• Capital budget — The capital budget IS a type (ventilators, monitors, building works).

⭐ Key point: Other budget approaches: zero-based budgeting (justify every item from zero), incremental budgeting (add a percentage to last year's budget), and flexible budgeting.$x$),
(181, $x$According to adult learning principles (andragogy), adults learn best when the content is:$x$, $x$Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience
Malcolm Knowles' principles of adult learning (andragogy) say adults learn best when learning is RELEVANT to their work or life, PROBLEM-CENTRED (solving real problems) and builds on their EXPERIENCE. Adults are self-directed, motivated by internal factors, and want to know WHY they need to learn something. Use case studies, discussion, simulation and hands-on practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Abstract, theoretical, and disconnected from real experience — Abstract theory disconnected from experience is less effective for adults.
• Delivered purely through passive lecture with no interaction — Passive lectures with no interaction ignore adults' experience and self-direction.
• Identical regardless of the learner's background — Adults differ; teaching should consider each learner's background.

⭐ Key point: Pedagogy = teaching children (teacher-directed). Andragogy = teaching adults (self-directed, experience-based).$x$),
(182, $x$A method of Teaching includes all the following Except....$x$, $x$Tertiary$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tertiary
The question asks which is NOT a teaching method. "Tertiary" means third level (as in tertiary education or tertiary hospitals); it is not a method of teaching. Teaching methods include explanation, lecture, demonstration (with return demonstration), discussion, role play, simulation, case study, tutorials and e-learning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Explanation — Explanation IS a teaching method.
• Lecturing — Lecturing IS a teaching method (good for large groups and knowledge).
• Demonstration — Demonstration IS a teaching method (best for practical skills).

⭐ Key point: For skills, use demonstration and return demonstration. For attitudes, use discussion and role play. For knowledge, use lectures, reading and e-learning.$x$),
(183, $x$Evaluation that occurs continuously throughout the implementation of care (e.g., during each shift) is called:$x$, $x$Formative (ongoing) evaluation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Formative (ongoing) evaluation
FORMATIVE evaluation happens CONTINUOUSLY during care, for example each shift: checking the patient's response and adjusting the plan as you go. It "forms" the care while it is happening. In nursing, ongoing reassessment (such as pain scores after analgesia) is formative evaluation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Summative evaluation — Summative evaluation happens at the END (for example at discharge) to judge the final outcome.
• Structure evaluation — Structure evaluation looks at resources (staff, equipment, buildings), not ongoing patient care.
• Terminal evaluation — Terminal evaluation is another name for evaluation at the end, which is summative.

⭐ Key point: Formative = during (to improve). Summative = at the end (to judge the result).$x$),
(184, $x$An incident report should be completed when:$x$, $x$A medication error occurs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A medication error occurs
An incident report is completed for any event that caused, or could have caused, harm to a patient, visitor or staff member, or that is not consistent with routine care. A MEDICATION ERROR is a classic example. Others: falls, needlestick injuries, equipment failures, wrong-patient procedures and near misses. The report is used to learn and improve the system, not to blame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient is discharged — Discharge is routine and needs no incident report.
• A nurse resumes duty — Resuming duty is routine.
• The ward is cleaned — Cleaning the ward is routine.

⭐ Key point: The incident report is NOT placed in the patient's notes, and the notes don't say "incident form completed"; record the facts of what happened and the care given.$x$),
(185, $x$The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN), as a professional regulatory body, is primarily responsible for:$x$, $x$Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline
The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN) is the government body that regulates nursing and midwifery in Nigeria. Its roles: setting and monitoring standards of nursing and midwifery education, accrediting schools, conducting licensing examinations (including post-basic council exams), registering and licensing nurses and midwives, renewing licences, and DISCIPLINING professional misconduct to protect the public.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only organising nursing conferences — Organising conferences is not its main legal function.
• Managing hospital construction budgets — Hospital construction budgets are not its responsibility.
• Setting national drug prices — Drug prices are not set by the NMCN.

⭐ Key point: The professional association and union for nurses is NANNM (National Association of Nigerian Nurses and Midwives), which is different from the regulator, the NMCN.$x$),
(186, $x$The doctrine of 'Res Ipsa Loquitur' ('the thing speaks for itself') applies when:$x$, $x$An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone's negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone's negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)
Res ipsa loquitur ("the thing speaks for itself") is a legal rule used when the injury is of a kind that would NOT normally happen without negligence, the thing that caused it was under the professional's control, and the patient did not contribute. A swab or instrument left inside after surgery is the classic example. The patient doesn't have to prove exactly how the negligence happened.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient explicitly consents to a risky procedure — A patient consenting to a risky procedure doesn't apply here; consent relates to known risks, not negligence.
• A nurse documents care accurately — Accurate documentation is good practice and not related to this doctrine.
• A patient refuses treatment — A patient's refusal of treatment is about autonomy and consent.

⭐ Key point: Prevent such events with surgical safety checklists, swab and instrument counts, and correct-site marking.$x$),
(187, $x$The four elements that must be proven to establish nursing negligence are:$x$, $x$Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages
To prove negligence, all FOUR elements must be shown. 1) DUTY of care existed (a nurse–patient relationship). 2) BREACH of that duty (care fell below the expected standard). 3) CAUSATION (the breach directly caused the harm). 4) DAMAGES (actual harm or loss occurred). If any element is missing, negligence is not proven.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consent, capacity, competence, and cost — Consent, capacity, competence and cost relate to consent and resources, not negligence.
• Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice — Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice are ETHICAL principles.
• Assessment, diagnosis, planning, and evaluation — Assessment, diagnosis, planning and evaluation are steps of the NURSING PROCESS.

⭐ Key point: Good documentation, following policies, staying within your scope and keeping up to date are the nurse's best protection against negligence claims.$x$),
(188, $x$'Battery' in a legal/nursing context refers to:$x$, $x$Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent
Battery is unlawful, intentional TOUCHING of a person without their consent, even if no harm is done and even if the touching was meant to help. Examples: giving an injection, inserting a catheter or doing a procedure when the patient has refused or not consented. Assault is threatening to touch, causing fear of being touched.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A device that powers medical equipment — A battery that powers equipment is the everyday meaning, not the legal one.
• A type of medication error — A medication error is a different issue (possible negligence).
• A category of documentation — Battery is not a documentation category.

⭐ Key point: Always get valid consent before touching or treating a patient, and respect refusal. In emergencies with no capacity, act in the patient's best interests.$x$),
(189, $x$For informed consent to be legally valid, it must include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity
The question asks which is NOT needed for valid consent. If the adult patient has full capacity, only THEY can consent. A family member's approval cannot replace the patient's own decision. Valid consent needs adequate INFORMATION (risks, benefits, alternatives), CAPACITY (able to understand, retain, weigh and communicate) and VOLUNTARY agreement (no pressure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adequate information given to the patient — Adequate information IS required.
• Patient capacity to understand the information — Patient capacity IS required.
• Voluntary agreement, free from coercion — Voluntary agreement IS required.

⭐ Key point: Family can be involved and consulted, but they cannot consent for or overrule a competent adult. Consent for children is given by parents or guardians.$x$),
(190, $x$'Veracity' as an ethical principle in nursing means:$x$, $x$Truthfulness and honesty with patients$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Truthfulness and honesty with patients
VERACITY means TRUTHFULNESS: being honest with patients, not lying or deceiving them, and giving information they need to make decisions. It builds trust and supports autonomy (informed consent). It also means being honest when errors occur (duty of candour).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments — Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments is FIDELITY.
• Fairness in resource distribution — Fairness in distributing resources is JUSTICE.
• Avoiding harm — Avoiding harm is NON-MALEFICENCE.

⭐ Key point: Ethical principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, veracity (truth), fidelity (keeping promises) and confidentiality.$x$),
(191, $x$A Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNR) order should be:$x$, $x$Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team
A DNR (DNACPR) order must be CLEARLY DOCUMENTED in the patient's record (on the proper form, signed and dated by the senior doctor responsible) and COMMUNICATED to the whole team at handover, so everyone knows not to start CPR. It should be discussed with the patient (if they have capacity) and/or the family, and reviewed regularly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assumed automatically for all elderly patients — A DNR is never automatic for elderly patients; age alone is not a reason.
• Kept verbal only, never written — A verbal-only DNR is unsafe and legally risky.
• Decided solely by the nurse without physician or patient/family involvement — The nurse cannot decide alone; a senior doctor makes the decision with the patient or family.

⭐ Key point: DNR means no CPR only. All other care (pain relief, oxygen, feeding, antibiotics, dignity) continues unless separately decided.$x$),
(192, $x$The Good Samaritan act covers:$x$, $x$Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith
Good Samaritan laws protect people who give EMERGENCY care in GOOD FAITH, outside their job (such as at a roadside accident), from civil liability (being sued), as long as they act within their training, without payment, and without gross negligence. These laws encourage health workers and the public to help in emergencies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Residents of long-term care facilities — Residents of long-term care facilities are covered by care standards, not specifically this law.
• Passerby in car accidents — Passersby are covered only when they help in good faith; the law protects helpers, not people simply present.
• Nurses who choose not to provide care in an emergency — Refusing to help in an emergency is not protected, and it may raise ethical issues.

⭐ Key point: When helping at the roadside, stay within your skills, get consent if possible, hand over to emergency services, and document what you did afterwards.$x$),
(193, $x$If you make a mistake in a record how should it be corrected?$x$, $x$Put a single line through the record$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Put a single line through the record
To correct an error in a record, draw a SINGLE LINE through the wrong entry so it can still be read. Write "error" (or "written in error"), and add your initials or signature, the date and the time. Then write the correct information. This keeps the record honest and shows exactly what was changed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• With correction fluid — Correction fluid hides the original entry, which looks like falsification of records.
• Scribble it out — Scribbling out makes the original unreadable and looks like tampering.
• Tell your mentor — Telling your mentor alone does not correct the record legally.

⭐ Key point: Never erase, tear out pages, or leave blank spaces. For electronic records, use the system's correction or amendment function.$x$),
(194, $x$A conscious, competent adult patient refuses a blood transfusion on religious grounds despite life-threatening anaemia. The nurse's ethically and legally appropriate response is to:$x$, $x$Respect the patient's autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respect the patient's autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly
A conscious adult with capacity has the legal and ethical right to refuse ANY treatment, even life-saving treatment, including a blood transfusion for religious reasons (such as Jehovah's Witnesses). This respects AUTONOMY. The nurse makes sure the patient is fully informed of the risks, explores alternatives (iron, erythropoietin, cell salvage, tranexamic acid), and DOCUMENTS the refusal carefully. Care continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer the transfusion anyway, since the situation is life-threatening — Giving blood anyway would be battery and a violation of autonomy.
• Have a family member override the patient's decision — Family members cannot override a competent adult's decision.
• Discharge the patient immediately with no further care — Discharging the patient without care is abandonment; other care continues.

⭐ Key point: An advance decision (such as a "no blood" card) must also be respected. For children, the courts can authorise transfusion in an emergency.$x$),
(195, $x$Which of the following is a recognised advantage of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) over purely laboratory-based nutritional assessment?$x$, $x$It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be
Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) is a bedside nutrition assessment based on history (weight loss, intake, gut symptoms, function) and examination (loss of fat and muscle, oedema). It predicts nutrition-related outcomes well. Unlike serum albumin, it is NOT distorted by fluid shifts or acute inflammation: albumin falls in critical illness because of inflammation and dilution, not just malnutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It requires no clinician training — SGA DOES need training to do well.
• It does not require any patient history — SGA DEPENDS on the patient's history; it is not done without it.
• It replaces the need for anthropometric data entirely — SGA uses clinical examination of body composition; it does not replace all anthropometric data.

⭐ Key point: In critically ill patients, albumin and prealbumin are poor nutrition markers. Use clinical tools (SGA, NUTRIC score) instead.$x$),
(196, $x$A patient on TPN is at risk for refeeding syndrome. The nurse should be most vigilant for which of the following electrolyte imbalances?$x$, $x$Hypophosphatemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypophosphatemia
In refeeding syndrome, starved patients are suddenly given nutrition (enteral or TPN). Insulin rises and drives glucose, PHOSPHATE, potassium and magnesium into the cells. Serum phosphate can fall dangerously, causing muscle weakness (including the diaphragm, leading to breathing failure and failure to wean), heart failure, arrhythmias, confusion, seizures and death. HYPOPHOSPHATAEMIA is the hallmark.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatremia — Sodium can shift, but high sodium is not the typical refeeding problem.
• Hyperkalemia — Potassium FALLS (hypokalaemia), it doesn't rise.
• Hypocalcemia — Calcium can be affected but is not the key electrolyte in refeeding.

⭐ Key point: Prevent refeeding: identify at-risk patients (low BMI, little intake for 5 days or more, alcohol misuse), give thiamine before feeding, start slowly (10–20 kcal/kg/day), and check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily.$x$),
(197, $x$Enteral nutrition is generally preferred over parenteral nutrition when:$x$, $x$The gut is functioning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The gut is functioning
"If the gut works, use it." Enteral nutrition (via the gut, by mouth or tube) is preferred whenever the gastrointestinal tract is FUNCTIONING. It keeps the gut lining healthy, supports gut immunity, reduces infections and bacterial translocation, has fewer complications than TPN (no line infections, fewer metabolic problems), and is cheaper.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The gut has completely failed — If the gut has completely failed (obstruction, severe ileus, short bowel), parenteral nutrition is used.
• Never — parenteral is always preferred — Parenteral is NOT always preferred; it is the second choice.
• Only in paediatric patients — Enteral feeding is preferred in adults and children alike.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours in ICU when the patient is haemodynamically stable. Use parenteral nutrition only if enteral feeding is impossible or not enough.$x$),
(198, $x$A nasogastric tube's position should be confirmed before feeding by:$x$, $x$pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation
Before the first feed (and before each feed or drug), the NG tube position must be checked. Approved methods: aspirate gastric fluid and test its pH with pH paper (pH 5.5 or below means the stomach), or, if no aspirate or pH is above 5.5, get a CHEST X-RAY read by a competent person. The X-ray is the gold standard, especially in ventilated or unconscious patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Auscultation ('whoosh test') alone — The "whoosh test" (listening for air) is UNRELIABLE and must never be used; it can sound the same if the tube is in the lung.
• The patient's verbal comfort report only — The patient's comfort tells you nothing about position; tubes in the lung may cause no symptoms.
• Tube colour — The tube's colour does not show where it is.

⭐ Key point: Feeding through a misplaced NG tube (in the lung) is a "never event". Mark the tube's length at the nostril and recheck it before use.$x$),
(199, $x$Manual hyperinflation$x$, $x$Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients
Manual hyperinflation (MHI) is a chest physiotherapy technique used in VENTILATED (intubated) patients. The patient is disconnected and given slow, deep breaths with a manual bag (larger than normal tidal volume), followed by a short inspiratory hold and then a quick release. This mimics a cough, re-expands collapsed lung areas, and moves secretions up to be suctioned.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Involve the use of expiratory pause of 5-10 seconds — The hold is short (inspiratory pause of 2–3 seconds), not a 5–10 second expiratory pause.
• Is used to provide fast and slow expiratory breath — MHI uses a slow deep breath in and a FAST release, not "fast and slow expiratory breaths".
• Technique is not used in the management of patient's self-ventilating — It is used mainly in intubated patients, but this statement is weaker than the correct one, which describes its main use.

⭐ Key point: Avoid MHI in unstable patients, those with high PEEP needs, undrained pneumothorax, bronchospasm or raised ICP. Monitor BP and SpO₂ during it.$x$),
(200, $x$Electrotherapy equipment includes:$x$, $x$Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)
Electrotherapy uses electrical energy to treat patients, mainly for pain relief and muscle stimulation. TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) sends mild electrical pulses through skin pads. These block pain signals (the "gate control" theory) and may release endorphins. It is used for pain after surgery, in labour and in chronic pain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• MRI — MRI is a diagnostic imaging scanner using magnets, not a therapy.
• Mammogram — A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast for diagnosis.
• Spirometer — A spirometer measures lung volumes and flow (a diagnostic or monitoring tool).

⭐ Key point: Do not use TENS over a pacemaker, the front of the neck, broken skin or the pregnant abdomen (except approved labour use), or in patients with epilepsy (over the head).$x$)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
select count(distinct n) as "questions updated (should be 200)", count(*) as "rows changed" from upd;
